{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788299908711,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8cffdeba144da1b9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/plans/board.yaml","offset":40,"limit":220},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/plans/board.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n40: - id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n41:   issue: gh:154\n42:   title: 'Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling). Escalated after 4 commitless automated runs (2026-09-01): the worktree has real uncommitted progress across src/ast.rs, src/check/mod.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, src/parse.rs -- each run reviews the diff, confirms it looks right, then fires landing WITHOUT committing, so land-lane.sh skips it every time. A stale ESCALATION.md from an already-folded issue-150 decision also sits in the worktree unremoved. Do not redispatch until the issue-154-escalation round is ruled (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-154).'\n43:   kind: build\n44:   needs: [sink-kind-build]\n45:   prio: 3\n46:   status: delegated\n47: \n48: - id: dsv-partials-migration\n49:   issue: gh:136\n50:   title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n51:   kind: build\n52:   needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n53:   prio: 5\n54:   status: todo\n55: \n56: - id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n57:   issue: gh:93\n58:   title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n59:   kind: build\n60:   needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n61:   prio: 3\n62:   status: todo\n63: \n64: - id: variant-types-flip\n65:   issue: gh:165\n66:   title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n67:   kind: build\n68:   needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n69:   prio: 2\n70:   model: fable\n71:   status: todo\n72: \n73: - id: shell-out-build\n74:   issue: gh:158\n75:   title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n76:   kind: build\n77:   needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\n78:   status: todo\n79: \n80: - id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\n81:   title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Ask as a grill round.'\n82:   kind: decide\n83:   needs: []\n84:   prio: 1\n85:   status: todo\n86: \n87: - id: stdin-redesign-build\n88:   issue: gh:159\n89:   title: Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative, gh:159 -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build)\n90:   kind: build\n91:   needs: []\n92:   model: fable\n93:   status: delegated\n94: \n95: - id: trait-interface-research\n96:   title: 'Ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism. Research spike before design: scope the minimal checker mechanism (trait declaration, impl-for-type, static dispatch) and whether it truly requires user-facing generics or can start monomorphic (one impl per concrete type, no generic trait bounds). Write findings to plans/.'\n97:   kind: build\n98:   needs: []\n99:   prio: 3\n100:   status: delegated\n101: \n102: - id: trait-interface-design\n103:   title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research'\n104:   kind: decide\n105:   needs: [trait-interface-research]\n106:   status: todo\n107: \n108: - id: mutation-semantics-spike\n109:   title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n110:   kind: build\n111:   needs: []\n112:   prio: 4\n113:   status: todo\n114: \n115: - id: float-build\n116:   issue: gh:149\n117:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\n118:   kind: build\n119:   needs: []\n120:   status: delegated\n121: \n122: - id: benchmark-suite-build\n123:   title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n124:   kind: build\n125:   needs: []\n126:   prio: 4\n127:   status: todo\n128: \n129: - id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n130:   issue: gh:102\n131:   title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n132:   kind: build\n133:   needs: []\n134:   prio: 3\n135:   status: todo\n136: \n137: - id: lines-utf8-edge\n138:   issue: gh:102\n139:   title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n140:   kind: decide\n141:   needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n142:   status: todo\n143: \n144: - id: search-layer-build\n145:   title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n146:   kind: build\n147:   needs: []\n148:   status: todo\n149: \n150: - id: sort-by-max-by\n151:   title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n152:   kind: build\n153:   needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n154:   status: todo\n155: \n156: - id: param-destructure-build\n157:   title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n158:   kind: build\n159:   needs: []\n160:   status: todo\n161: \n162: - id: bigint-tracking\n163:   issue: gh:112\n164:   title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n165:   kind: build\n166:   needs: []\n167:   prio: 5\n168:   status: todo\n169: \n170: - id: js-node-web-split-build\n171:   issue: gh:160\n172:   title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n173:   kind: build\n174:   needs: []\n175:   status: todo\n176: \n177: - id: module-routing-syntax-build\n178:   issue: gh:167\n179:   title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n180:   kind: build\n181:   needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n182:   prio: 5\n183:   status: todo\n184: \n185: - id: erlang-target-consideration\n186:   issue: gh:163\n187:   title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n188:   kind: decide\n189:   needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n190:   prio: 5\n191:   status: todo\n192: \n193: - id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n194:   issue: gh:170\n195:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n196:   kind: build\n197:   needs: []\n198:   prio: 4\n199:   status: todo\n200: \n201: - id: variant-checker-capital-first\n202:   issue: gh:156\n203:   title: Checker rejects enum variant declarations that don't start with a capital letter, per the curly-only-pattern ruling (gh:156 ruling)\n204:   kind: build\n205:   needs: []\n206:   status: delegated\n207: \n208: - id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n209:   issue: gh:122\n210:   title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n211:   kind: build\n212:   needs: []\n213:   status: todo\n214: \n215: - id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n216:   issue: gh:119\n217:   title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n218:   kind: build\n219:   needs: []\n220:   prio: 3\n221:   status: todo\n222: \n223: - id: http-query-sugar-research\n224:   title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n225:   kind: build\n226:   needs: []\n227:   prio: 5\n228:   status: todo\n229: \n230: - id: partial-application-system-design\n231:   issue: gh:119\n232:   title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n233:   kind: decide\n234:   needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n235:   status: todo\n236: \n237: \n238: \n239: - id: draft-core-model-migration\n240:   title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n241:   kind: build\n242:   model: fable\n243:   needs: []\n244:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n245:   prio: 4\n246:   status: todo\n247: \n248: - id: draft-access-model-migration\n249:   title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n250:   kind: build\n251:   needs: []\n252:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n253:   prio: 4\n254:   status: todo\n255: \n256: - id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n257:   title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n258:   kind: build\n259:   needs: []\n\n(Showing lines 40-259 of 408. Use offset=260 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n  issue: gh:154\n  title: 'Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling). Escalated after 4 commitless automated runs (2026-09-01): the worktree has real uncommitted progress across src/ast.rs, src/check/mod.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, src/parse.rs -- each run reviews the diff, confirms it looks right, then fires landing WITHOUT committing, so land-lane.sh skips it every time. A stale ESCALATION.md from an already-folded issue-150 decision also sits in the worktree unremoved. Do not redispatch until the issue-154-escalation round is ruled (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-154).'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sink-kind-build]\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n  issue: gh:93\n  title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n  kind: build","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/plans/board.yaml","text":"- id: jsonlines-tail-marker-build\n  issue: gh:154\n  title: 'Implement the |> tail-pipeline marker for sink calls, restricted to outermost position (issue #9 oddity ruling). Escalated after 4 commitless automated runs (2026-09-01): the worktree has real uncommitted progress across src/ast.rs, src/check/mod.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, src/parse.rs -- each run reviews the diff, confirms it looks right, then fires landing WITHOUT committing, so land-lane.sh skips it every time. A stale ESCALATION.md from an already-folded issue-150 decision also sits in the worktree unremoved. Do not redispatch until the issue-154-escalation round is ruled (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-154).'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sink-kind-build]\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n  issue: gh:93\n  title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-types-flip\n  issue: gh:165\n  title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n  prio: 2\n  model: fable\n  status: todo\n\n- id: shell-out-build\n  issue: gh:158\n  title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\n  title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Ask as a grill round.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: []\n  prio: 1\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdin-redesign-build\n  issue: gh:159\n  title: Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative, gh:159 -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  model: fable\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: trait-interface-research\n  title: 'Ruled (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff): declared interface/trait mechanism (Rust/Swift-shaped) is the direction for ad hoc polymorphism. Research spike before design: scope the minimal checker mechanism (trait declaration, impl-for-type, static dispatch) and whether it truly requires user-facing generics or can start monomorphic (one impl per concrete type, no generic trait bounds). Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: trait-interface-design\n  title: 'Grill the concrete trait/impl syntax and dispatch mechanism (overloading-and-utf8-lines round ruling spinoff), informed by trait-interface-research'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [trait-interface-research]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: mutation-semantics-spike\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) across the backends\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\n  title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: lines-utf8-edge\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-layer-build\n  title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: sort-by-max-by\n  title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: param-destructure-build\n  title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: bigint-tracking\n  issue: gh:112\n  title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: js-node-web-split-build\n  issue: gh:160\n  title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: module-routing-syntax-build\n  issue: gh:167\n  title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-target-consideration\n  issue: gh:163\n  title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n  issue: gh:170\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-checker-capital-first\n  issue: gh:156\n  title: Checker rejects enum variant declarations that don't start with a capital letter, per the curly-only-pattern ruling (gh:156 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n  issue: gh:122\n  title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-research\n  title: 'Maintainer note (docs/.annotations/notes.json composed 2026-08-31T22:08:16Z, unscheduled): consider a simple HTTP query builtin (like Python requests) as thin sugar over whatever each backend runtime already provides, minimal added complexity. Research spike: survey what request/response building blocks already exist per backend (Go net/http, JS fetch, Python urllib, Lua, Rust reqwest, native) before scoping a design row. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: partial-application-system-design\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n  status: todo\n\n\n\n- id: draft-core-model-migration\n  title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  model: fable\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-access-model-migration\n  title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n  title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []","lineStart":40,"lineEnd":259,"totalLines":408,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/board.yaml","time":{"start":1788299908694,"end":1788299908710}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's no docs/.grill/ directory. Let me read the board.yaml more fully, around lines 60-70 and 195-230, and search for the rows mentioned in the brief. The brief mentions board.yaml's variant-checker-capital-first row and the docs/.grill/issue-156-land-failure.round.yaml file. Neither might exist in this worktree (cleared on reshape). Let me check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05efb087d001TsuTT5d94m7J7C","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efaf8f8001iU7AMbGbW17xM6"}}
