or ([unwrap](../reference/operators/unwrap.md)), or fold a default into the chain itself\ndocs/guides/matching.md:108:```toylang\ndocs/guides/matching.md:112:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:114:```output\ndocs/guides/matching.md:116:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:123:```toylang\ndocs/guides/matching.md:131:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:133:```output\ndocs/guides/matching.md:135:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:143:come back absent -- because the chain is no longer partial; every input now hits an arm:\ndocs/guides/matching.md:145:```toylang\ndocs/guides/matching.md:153:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:155:```output\ndocs/guides/matching.md:157:```\ndocs/guides/matching.md:162:```case\ndocs/guides/matching.md:164:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:13:```toylang\ndocs/guides/enums.md:20:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:22:```output\ndocs/guides/enums.md:24:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:27:system takes it from there: `flip input` below is a complete program that reads a status\ndocs/guides/enums.md:30:```case\ndocs/guides/enums.md:31:enum_input\ndocs/guides/enums.md:32:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:36:Because the enum declares a closed set, input validation has something to check against. A\ndocs/guides/enums.md:41:```case\ndocs/guides/enums.md:42:enum_scalar_input_reject\ndocs/guides/enums.md:43:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:55:```case\ndocs/guides/enums.md:57:```\ndocs/guides/enums.md:61:Enums compose with streams: `inputs` typed as `Stream<Msg>` validates each line against the\ndocs/guides/enums.md:65:```case\ndocs/guides/enums.md:67:```\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n \"toylang|error|output|refuses|input|case|^\\`\\`\\`\" docs/guides/enums.md docs/guides/matching.md docs/reference/operators/comparison.md docs/reference/types/enum.md docs/tutorial/04-enums.md docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","time":{"start":1788299991218,"end":1788299991221}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the docs test. The task is: gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases in the doc-corpus test toylang::docs every_fragment_is_a_real_program.\n\nThe flagged cases:\n- BROKEN (7 backends each) - these are fragments that currently compile-fail with the new checker rule. There are 6 locations:\n  - docs/guides/enums.md:13 (ping of Msg)\n  - docs/guides/matching.md:66 (a of S)\n  - docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28 (point of Shape)\n  - docs/reference/types/enum.md:7 (point of Shape)\n  - docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6 (active of Status)\n  - docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20 (point of Shape)\n  - docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40 (point of Shape)\n\nWait, the brief says: \"6 doc pages still declare lowercase-leading variants\" — let me count: guides/enums.md:13, guides/matching.md:66, reference/operators/comparison.md:28, reference/types/enum.md:7, tutorial/04-enums.md:6/20/40. That's 5 distinct pages but 7 fragments (04-enums.md has 3). The brief says \"6 doc pages\" but lists: enums.md, matching.md, comparison.md, enum.md, 04-enums.md = 5 pages. Hmm, maybe counting differently. Anyway, I'll gate by exact location.\n\n- MISQUOTED (4 fixtures):\n  - docs/guides/matching.md:54\n  - docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\n  - docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\n  - docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\n\nThe task: \"gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases in the doc-corpus test toylang::docs every_fragment_is_a_real_program ... so `just test` goes green with the checker enforcement intact. A separate board row (doc-migrate-capital-variants) will do the actual content migration and remove this gating later -- leave a comment at the gate pointing to that row.\"\n\nWait — but there's a nuance. The brief says \"do NOT migrate the doc content\". So I need to add gating/xfail logic in tests/docs.rs that identifies exactly these flagged fragments and skips the failure (or skips them entirely), with a comment pointing to the doc-migrate-capital-variants board row.\n\nLet me look at how the fragments identify themselves: `f.at` is \"page:line\" of the opening fence, e.g. \"docs/guides/enums.md:13\". So I can gate by (page, line) pairs.\n\nNow, let me consider how to design the gate. Options:\n1. Skip the fragment entirely (don't run any check). This is a \"silent skip\" which the docs.rs module docstring explicitly criticizes (\"A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed.\"). So a silent skip is against the repo's philosophy.\n2. Replace the expected outcome check with a weaker but still real check — e.g. for BROKEN fragments, assert that the program does NOT compile (which it currently fails to do), rather than requiring it to compile and run. That's a real claim: \"this fragment does not currently compile; it is expected to be migrated\". For MISQUOTED fragments (which have `error` fences), the checker message changed. So currently the page says one message, the checker says another. We can't gate the MISQUOTED check by just asserting the new message because then the page content would be wrong (migrating would fix that). Actually the point of gating is: the doc content is stale, migration will fix it. We want `just test` green now.\n\nHmm, but the brief says \"gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases\". So the intent is a gate: mark these specific fragments so the test doesn't fail on them, but leave a comment at the gate pointing to the migration row.\n\nLet me think about the best approach consistent with the repo philosophy. The docs.rs module docstring rails against silent skips. But this is explicitly a temporary gate with a pointer to a migration row. The best design:\n\nFor each gated fragment, rather than silently skipping, we can still assert something meaningful:\n- For BROKEN (output/refuses fragments that now fail to compile): we can assert that the program fails to compile (with the checker's new error), keeping the check meaningful (\"the program does not compile\" is still a real claim — it pins the current state). Actually we could check `toylang::compile` errors. That keeps it from being a total no-op.\n- For MISQUOTED (error fragments where the checker message no longer matches the page): we can assert that the program fails to compile (it does — that's why MISQUOTED fired, actually no — MISQUOTED fires when compile returns Err but message != want. So it does fail to compile). So we can assert it fails to compile, acknowledging the exact message may change post-migration.\n\nHmm, but that's adding complexity. Let me re-read the brief: \"gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases in the doc-corpus test toylang::docs every_fragment_is_a_real_program (docs/guides/enums.md:13, ...) so `just test` goes green with the checker enforcement intact. ... leave a comment at the gate pointing to that row.\"\n\nSo a straightforward \"xfail\" approach: a list of `(page, line)` locations that are known-broken; for those, the test tolerates the failure instead of asserting. The comment at the gate points to board row `doc-migrate-capital-variants`.\n\nLet me think about what \"with the checker enforcement intact\" means. The variant-casing checker rule (capital-first variant names) is enforced in the compiler. We must NOT weaken the checker. We only make the docs test tolerate these specific stale fragments. Good.\n\nNow, how does the migration row get named? The brief references \"board row doc-migrate-capital-variants\". Let me check the board for that row — it wasn't in the grep results. Let me search the board again for \"doc-migrate\".\n\nThe grep for \"doc-migrate-capital-variants\" returned nothing in board.yaml (only the two rows variant-types-flip and variant-checker-capital-first matched \"capital-first\"). So the doc-migrate-capital-variants row doesn't exist yet in this board? The brief says \"A separate board row (doc-migrate-capital-variants) will do the actual content migration and remove this gating later\". So the row name is doc-migrate-capital-variants. Maybe I should add that row to the board so it's real and the comment can point to a real thing. But the brief says \"do not touch plans/\". Hmm.\n\n\"Hard constraints: ... do not touch plans/.\"\n\nSo I can't add the row to the board. The comment at the gate should just reference the row by name \"doc-migrate-capital-variants\". That's fine — the comment points to the row that will exist.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"A separate board row (doc-migrate-capital-variants) will do the actual content migration and remove this gating later -- leave a comment at the gate pointing to that row.\"\n\nSo I leave a comment naming the row. Since I can't touch plans/, I just reference it by name.\n\nNow let me design the gating. Let me look at the actual doc pages to understand context and line numbers.\n\nLet me check git log for my prior commits to see what was already done (checker rule). Also check the current state of the checker and tests/variant_casing.rs.\n\nLet me look at the docs files first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05efc4f36001ZvtrnzGvmqkAdD","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efb4ded001aTECN1RHbEOeMg"}}
