{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300068022,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a154ff708c276aa3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/enums.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/enums.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Typing wire data with enums\n2: \n3: The task: stdin carries JSON whose shape varies by kind -- status strings, tagged messages,\n4: mixed events -- and the program must handle every kind, provably.\n5: \n6: ## Name the set\n7: \n8: Wire formats spell \"one of a known set\" two ways: a bare string (`\"active\"`), or a\n9: single-key wrapper (`{\"text\": {\"body\": \"hi\"}}`). An enum declaration covers both at once,\n10: because that pair is exactly the enum encoding: unit variants are strings, payload variants\n11: are wrappers.\n12: \n13: ```toylang\n14: enum Msg { ping, quit, text{body: Str} }\n15: \n16: fn render(m: Msg) -> Str =\n17:     m | ping -> \"*ping*\" or quit -> \"*quit*\" or text -> .body\n18: \n19: render(text({body: \"hi\"}))\n20: ```\n21: \n22: ```output\n23: hi\n24: ```\n25: \n26: Declare the variants after the strings and wrappers the wire actually carries, and the type\n27: system takes it from there: `flip input` below is a complete program that reads a status\n28: and answers with the other one.\n29: \n30: ```case\n31: enum_input\n32: ```\n33: \n34: ## Let validation refuse the garbage\n35: \n36: Because the enum declares a closed set, input validation has something to check against. A\n37: string that names no variant, a wrapper whose payload misses the declared type -- both are\n38: refused before the program runs, on every backend, rather than flowing through as\n39: unmatched data:\n40: \n41: ```case\n42: enum_scalar_input_reject\n43: ```\n44: \n45: This is the point of typing the wire: the match inside the program never meets a value\n46: outside the set, which is what lets the checker demand every variant be handled and nothing\n47: else.\n48: \n49: ## Payloads are any single type\n50: \n51: A record payload (`text{body: Str}`) is declared in braces and destructured in the arm. A\n52: scalar or `Vec` payload is declared in parens, constructed like a call, and bound whole to\n53: `.` in its arm:\n54: \n55: ```case\n56: enum_vec_payload\n57: ```\n58: \n59: ## The whole shape at stream scale\n60: \n61: Enums compose with streams: `inputs` typed as `Stream<Msg>` validates each line against the\n62: set as it arrives, one exhaustive match inside `map` proves coverage, and the sink prints\n63: as it goes:\n64: \n65: ```case\n66: jsonlines_enum_stream\n67: ```\n68: \n69: A default arm (`any() -> ...`) trades some of that proof away for brevity; reach for it\n70: when new variants should fall through rather than break the build. A guard arm trades none\n71: of it away and cannot substitute for `any()` either -- the [matching guide](matching.md)\n72: covers why. The [match reference](../reference/operators/match.md) has the arm shapes.\n\n(End of file - total 72 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Typing wire data with enums\n\nThe task: stdin carries JSON whose shape varies by kind -- status strings, tagged messages,\nmixed events -- and the program must handle every kind, provably.\n\n## Name the set\n\nWire formats spell \"one of a known set\" two ways: a bare string (`\"active\"`), or a\nsingle-key wrapper (`{\"text\": {\"body\": \"hi\"}}`). An enum declaration covers both at once,\nbecause that pair is exactly the enum encoding: unit variants are strings, payload variants\nare wrappers.\n\n```toylang\nenum Msg { ping, quit, text{body: Str} }\n\nfn render(m: Msg) -> Str =\n    m | ping -> \"*ping*\" or quit -> \"*quit*\" or text -> .body\n\nrender(text({body: \"hi\"}))\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/enums.md","text":"# Typing wire data with enums\n\nThe task: stdin carries JSON whose shape varies by kind -- status strings, tagged messages,\nmixed events -- and the program must handle every kind, provably.\n\n## Name the set\n\nWire formats spell \"one of a known set\" two ways: a bare string (`\"active\"`), or a\nsingle-key wrapper (`{\"text\": {\"body\": \"hi\"}}`). An enum declaration covers both at once,\nbecause that pair is exactly the enum encoding: unit variants are strings, payload variants\nare wrappers.\n\n```toylang\nenum Msg { ping, quit, text{body: Str} }\n\nfn render(m: Msg) -> Str =\n    m | ping -> \"*ping*\" or quit -> \"*quit*\" or text -> .body\n\nrender(text({body: \"hi\"}))\n```\n\n```output\nhi\n```\n\nDeclare the variants after the strings and wrappers the wire actually carries, and the type\nsystem takes it from there: `flip input` below is a complete program that reads a status\nand answers with the other one.\n\n```case\nenum_input\n```\n\n## Let validation refuse the garbage\n\nBecause the enum declares a closed set, input validation has something to check against. A\nstring that names no variant, a wrapper whose payload misses the declared type -- both are\nrefused before the program runs, on every backend, rather than flowing through as\nunmatched data:\n\n```case\nenum_scalar_input_reject\n```\n\nThis is the point of typing the wire: the match inside the program never meets a value\noutside the set, which is what lets the checker demand every variant be handled and nothing\nelse.\n\n## Payloads are any single type\n\nA record payload (`text{body: Str}`) is declared in braces and destructured in the arm. A\nscalar or `Vec` payload is declared in parens, constructed like a call, and bound whole to\n`.` in its arm:\n\n```case\nenum_vec_payload\n```\n\n## The whole shape at stream scale\n\nEnums compose with streams: `inputs` typed as `Stream<Msg>` validates each line against the\nset as it arrives, one exhaustive match inside `map` proves coverage, and the sink prints\nas it goes:\n\n```case\njsonlines_enum_stream\n```\n\nA default arm (`any() -> ...`) trades some of that proof away for brevity; reach for it\nwhen new variants should fall through rather than break the build. A guard arm trades none\nof it away and cannot substitute for `any()` either -- the [matching guide](matching.md)\ncovers why. The [match reference](../reference/operators/match.md) has the arm shapes.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":72,"totalLines":72,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/guides/enums.md","time":{"start":1788300068012,"end":1788300068020}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The reshape commit db0c6a2 is on main. This branch (issue-156) doesn't contain it. So the board on THIS branch still shows variant-checker-capital-first as \"delegated\" without the doc-migrate-capital-variants row. Since I'm told not to touch plans/, I'll leave the board as-is. But the brief says the doc-migrate-capital-variants row exists. On this worktree it doesn't, but that's fine — my job is only to gate the doc cases and reference the row by name in a comment.\n\nLet me verify what the board on main looks like (the reshape). Actually, my worktree's board is what matters for this lane; I shouldn't touch plans/. The comment in tests/docs.rs just references the row name doc-migrate-capital-variants.\n\nNow, let me understand the current checker and the failing docs fragments. Let me run `just check` first to confirm the current state (the fast loop skips the docs mega-test). Then run the specific docs test to confirm failures. Actually the fast loop is `just check` which excludes the docs test. To see the docs test failures, I'd run `cargo nextest run -E 'test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'`.\n\nLet me first look at the relevant doc pages to understand the fragments, then look at how the test identifies them (f.at = \"page:line\" of opening fence).\n\nThe flagged locations (from the brief and LAND-FAILURE.txt):\nBROKEN (7 backends each) — these fragments compile-fail:\n1. docs/guides/enums.md:13 (toylang fence at line 13) — Msg/ping\n2. docs/guides/matching.md:66 (toylang fence at line 66) — S/a\n3. docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28 (toylang fence at line 28) — Shape/point\n4. docs/reference/types/enum.md:7 (toylang fence at line 7) — Shape/point\n5. docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6 (toylang fence at line 6) — Status/active\n6. docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20 (toylang fence at line 20) — Shape/point\n7. docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40 (toylang fence at line 40) — Shape/point\n\nMISQUOTED — error fragments where the checker message changed:\n1. docs/guides/matching.md:54 (toylang fence at line 54, error fence at 60) — page says \"a match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b`\" but checker says variant casing error\n2. docs/reference/types/enum.md:34 (toylang fence at line 34, error fence at 42) — page says match coverage but checker says casing\n3. docs/reference/types/enum.md:67 (toylang fence at line 67, error fence at 73) — page says \"type `Json` is written in terms of itself\" but checker says casing\n4. docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67 (toylang fence at line 67, error fence at 73) — page says match coverage but checker says casing\n\nNote for the MISQUOTED cases: the checker currently rejects the program with a variant-casing error (a different error than what the page claims). So these programs don't even reach the error the page describes. After migration, the program would compile past the casing check, and then the page's claimed error (match coverage / self-reference) may or may not be the actual error. So these are genuinely stale fixtures.\n\nNow the task: gate or xfail exactly these cases in the docs test so `just test` goes green with the checker enforcement intact.\n\nDesign considerations:\n\nThe docs.rs module docstring explicitly rails against silent skips (\"A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed\"). So a pure skip would be against the repo philosophy. But the brief explicitly says \"gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases ... so just test goes green ... leave a comment at the gate pointing to that row.\" So gating is sanctioned here. But I should make the gate as honest as possible.\n\nOptions for the gate mechanism:\n\nOption A: A list of known-broken (page, line) locations. For each fragment whose `at` matches, the test tolerates the failure rather than recording it. This is a \"skip list\". It silently skips — the module docstring criticizes silent skips.\n\nOption B: A list of known-broken locations; for each, instead of the normal outcome check, assert the *actual current* state: for BROKEN fragments, assert that the program does not compile (keeping it real: it pins that the fragment is currently broken). For MISQUOTED fragments, assert that the program does not compile (pinning the casing rejection). This keeps the check meaningful without requiring content migration.\n\nOption B is better: it doesn't silently skip; it still verifies a claim (the fragment is currently rejected by the checker, pending migration). And when the migration happens and removes the gate, the full outcome check returns. Also, Option B gives a clear signal: when migration lands and these fragments start compiling/printing, the \"assert does not compile\" check would FAIL, alerting that the gate must be removed. That's exactly what we want: the gate is a tripwire that goes red the moment migration makes it stale.\n\nActually wait — there's a subtlety with the MISQUOTED ones. Currently MISQUOTED fires because compile returns Err but the message differs from the page. With Option B, we assert compile errors (any message). After migration, the program may compile fine (if the page's described error no longer occurs) or may still error with the page's claimed message (if it does). Either way the \"assert fails to compile\" tripwire goes red when migration changes behavior — good.\n\nHmm, but actually should the gate keep asserting that these fragments still fail to compile? Let me think about what \"the checker enforcement intact\" means. The checker must still reject lowercase variants. The docs test's normal path for these fragments would currently fail. Gating means: don't run the normal expectation for these; instead run a weaker expectation that's still real.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually the simplest honest gate: keep these fragments running through `toylang::compile` and assert that they are rejected — i.e., the gate verifies \"the fragment does not compile yet (it is pending migration)\". For the BROKEN output/refuses fragments, the current failure is that they fail to compile (BROKEN ... could not run: a variant name...). So asserting \"does not compile\" matches the current state exactly.\n\nFor the MISQUOTED error fragments, the current failure is that the compile error message doesn't match the page. Asserting \"does not compile\" (regardless of message) matches current state.\n\nThis design:\n1. Keeps `just test` green.\n2. Keeps checker enforcement intact (compiler unchanged).\n3. Is not a silent skip — it still runs the compiler on these programs and asserts a true claim.\n4. Acts as a tripwire: when the migration row removes the gating, these assertions must be removed too; if a fragment starts compiling before the gate is removed, the test goes red (visible).\n5. Leaves a comment pointing to the doc-migrate-capital-variants row.\n\nWait, but there's a question: does gating in tests/docs.rs make the whole fragment skip the 7-backend agreement run? Yes — for gated fragments we replace the outcome check entirely. That's the point (they can't run yet).\n\nLet me now look at the exact structure. I'll add a function `is_capital_variant_gate(at)` or a list, and in the main loop, if gated, run the weak check.\n\nLet me define the gate precisely. The `at` strings are:\n- \"docs/guides/enums.md:13\"\n- \"docs/guides/matching.md:66\"\n- \"docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28\"\n- \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:7\"\n- \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6\"\n- \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20\"\n- \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40\"\n- \"docs/guides/matching.md:54\"\n- \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\"\n- \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\"\n- \"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\"\n\nI should match the opening fence line, not the error/output fence line. Let me verify: LAND-FAILURE.txt reports \"docs/guides/matching.md:54: ... the page says 'a match over `S`...' (at byte 23)\". The page's error fence is at line 60, toylang fence at 54. The fragment's `at` is the toylang fence line (54). Good — the failures report `f.at`, which is the toylang opening fence line. So my gate list should use those same `at` strings.\n\nLet me confirm by reading the actual pages.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05efd835c001Su45V17d4AQzj3","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efcab58001Nh9rOWzx0jo9se"}}
