{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300068715,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b736eb0fb6f42a1f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/matching.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/matching.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Choosing a branching form\n2: \n3: The task: a program branches -- on a condition, on which variant of an enum arrived, or on\n4: whether anything applies at all. All three go through match; which arm shape fits depends on\n5: the data.\n6: \n7: ## Guard chains\n8: \n9: A guard chain (`cond -> body or cond -> body or ... or default`) is the whole of conditional\n10: logic: each branch is a `cond -> body` arm, and the final bare expression is the default. It\n11: holds up well once a cascade grows past two or three branches, because each condition sits\n12: next to its own result instead of the cascade nesting further:\n13: \n14: ```toylang\n15: fn shipping(kg: Int) -> Int =\n16:     kg | . <= 1 -> 5 or . <= 5 -> 12 or . <= 20 -> 25 or 40\n17: \n18: {a: shipping(1), b: shipping(3), c: shipping(20), d: shipping(25)}\n19: ```\n20: \n21: ```output\n22: {\"a\":5,\"b\":12,\"c\":25,\"d\":40}\n23: ```\n24: \n25: ## Testing two things at once\n26: \n27: A guard is an ordinary Bool expression, so [`and`, `or`, and\n28: `not`](../reference/operators/boolean.md) compose one out of several tests. `or` inside a\n29: guard is disjunction rather than the arm separator -- an arm's left side is still being read,\n30: so the chain has not reached the point where an `or` could end it:\n31: \n32: ```toylang\n33: fn band(n: Int) -> Str =\n34:     n | . < 0 or . > 100 -> \"out\" or . >= 50 and . <= 60 -> \"mid\" or \"in\"\n35: \n36: {a: band(-1), b: band(55), c: band(20)}\n37: ```\n38: \n39: ```output\n40: {\"a\":\"out\",\"b\":\"mid\",\"c\":\"in\"}\n41: ```\n42: \n43: An arm's *body* is the one place the separator wins, so a Bool `or` written there needs\n44: parens: `. > 3 -> (. == 5 or . == 6)`. Nothing else about the connectives changes inside a\n45: chain.\n46: \n47: ## Matching enums\n48: \n49: The [enums guide](enums.md) covers typing wire data and matching by variant end to end. One\n50: thing worth knowing going in: a guard arm can sit in the same chain as variant arms, but it\n51: never finishes the job. Coverage is checked on the pattern, not the runtime value, so even a\n52: guard that always holds does not close the match:\n53: \n54: ```toylang\n55: enum S { a, b }\n56: \n57: S.a | a -> 1 or 1 == 1 -> 2\n58: ```\n59: \n60: ```error\n61: a match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b` (at byte 23)\n62: ```\n63: \n64: Name the remaining variant, or end the chain in `any()`:\n65: \n66: ```toylang\n67: enum S { a, b }\n68: \n69: S.a | a -> 1 or any() -> 2\n70: ```\n71: \n72: ```output\n73: 1\n74: ```\n75: \n76: ## Partial chains and their Opt results\n77: \n78: A guard chain with no default is a legitimate way to say \"some inputs don't classify\" --\n79: declining every arm produces `Opt`, not a refusal:\n80: \n81: ```toylang\n82: fn discount(total: Int) -> Opt<Int> = total | . >= 100 -> total / 10\n83: \n84: {a: discount(150), b: discount(50)}\n85: ```\n86: \n87: ```output\n88: {\"a\":15,\"b\":null}\n89: ```\n90: \n91: The declared `Opt<T>` return reaches each arm as `T`, the same as any other declared type,\n92: so a bare `[]` resolves inside a partial arm without needing a variable to infer it from:\n93: \n94: ```toylang\n95: fn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> []\n96: \n97: {a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n98: ```\n99: \n100: ```output\n101: {\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n102: ```\n103: \n104: Consume the result the way any `Opt` is consumed: `!` if absence should become a runtime\n105: error ([unwrap](../reference/operators/unwrap.md)), or fold a default into the chain itself\n106: if it should not:\n107: \n108: ```toylang\n109: fn discount(total: Int) -> Int = total | . >= 100 -> total / 10 or 0\n110: \n111: {a: discount(150), b: discount(50)}\n112: ```\n113: \n114: ```output\n115: {\"a\":15,\"b\":0}\n116: ```\n117: \n118: A partial chain whose arms are themselves `Opt`-typed doubles the wrapping: declining the\n119: chain is one absence, matching and finding nothing is another, and since absence is tagged\n120: in memory they stay two different values -- `none` and `some(none)`. Note the doubled\n121: return type:\n122: \n123: ```toylang\n124: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n125:     entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9]\n126: \n127: {\n128:     a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n129:     b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [5]})\n130: }\n131: ```\n132: \n133: ```output\n134: {\"a\":null,\"b\":null}\n135: ```\n136: \n137: Both *print* `null`, because serialization flattens every level of tagging away -- it is\n138: lossy about this the way it is about every type-level distinction. The program can still\n139: tell them apart: `!` peels exactly one level, so unwrapping `b` yields the inner absence\n140: and prints `null`, while unwrapping `a` stops the program.\n141: \n142: A default arm collapses the doubling back to one level -- even a default that can itself\n143: come back absent -- because the chain is no longer partial; every input now hits an arm:\n144: \n145: ```toylang\n146: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n147:     entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9]\n148: \n149: {\n150:     a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n151:     b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n152: }\n153: ```\n154: \n155: ```output\n156: {\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n157: ```\n158: \n159: The same chain also runs directly per element inside `map`, guards and field reads over `.`\n160: with no named function in between:\n161: \n162: ```case\n163: map_guard_chain_over_vec_field\n164: ```\n165: \n166: The [match reference](../reference/operators/match.md) has the full arm-shape rundown; the\n167: [matching tutorial chapter](../tutorial/06-matching.md) is the narrative version.\n\n(End of file - total 167 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Choosing a branching form\n\nThe task: a program branches -- on a condition, on which variant of an enum arrived, or on\nwhether anything applies at all. All three go through match; which arm shape fits depends on\nthe data.\n\n## Guard chains\n\nA guard chain (`cond -> body or cond -> body or ... or default`) is the whole of conditional\nlogic: each branch is a `cond -> body` arm, and the final bare expression is the default. It\nholds up well once a cascade grows past two or three branches, because each condition sits\nnext to its own result instead of the cascade nesting further:\n\n```toylang\nfn shipping(kg: Int) -> Int =\n    kg | . <= 1 -> 5 or . <= 5 -> 12 or . <= 20 -> 25 or 40\n\n{a: shipping(1), b: shipping(3), c: shipping(20), d: shipping(25)}\n```\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/guides/matching.md","text":"# Choosing a branching form\n\nThe task: a program branches -- on a condition, on which variant of an enum arrived, or on\nwhether anything applies at all. All three go through match; which arm shape fits depends on\nthe data.\n\n## Guard chains\n\nA guard chain (`cond -> body or cond -> body or ... or default`) is the whole of conditional\nlogic: each branch is a `cond -> body` arm, and the final bare expression is the default. It\nholds up well once a cascade grows past two or three branches, because each condition sits\nnext to its own result instead of the cascade nesting further:\n\n```toylang\nfn shipping(kg: Int) -> Int =\n    kg | . <= 1 -> 5 or . <= 5 -> 12 or . <= 20 -> 25 or 40\n\n{a: shipping(1), b: shipping(3), c: shipping(20), d: shipping(25)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":5,\"b\":12,\"c\":25,\"d\":40}\n```\n\n## Testing two things at once\n\nA guard is an ordinary Bool expression, so [`and`, `or`, and\n`not`](../reference/operators/boolean.md) compose one out of several tests. `or` inside a\nguard is disjunction rather than the arm separator -- an arm's left side is still being read,\nso the chain has not reached the point where an `or` could end it:\n\n```toylang\nfn band(n: Int) -> Str =\n    n | . < 0 or . > 100 -> \"out\" or . >= 50 and . <= 60 -> \"mid\" or \"in\"\n\n{a: band(-1), b: band(55), c: band(20)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"out\",\"b\":\"mid\",\"c\":\"in\"}\n```\n\nAn arm's *body* is the one place the separator wins, so a Bool `or` written there needs\nparens: `. > 3 -> (. == 5 or . == 6)`. Nothing else about the connectives changes inside a\nchain.\n\n## Matching enums\n\nThe [enums guide](enums.md) covers typing wire data and matching by variant end to end. One\nthing worth knowing going in: a guard arm can sit in the same chain as variant arms, but it\nnever finishes the job. Coverage is checked on the pattern, not the runtime value, so even a\nguard that always holds does not close the match:\n\n```toylang\nenum S { a, b }\n\nS.a | a -> 1 or 1 == 1 -> 2\n```\n\n```error\na match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b` (at byte 23)\n```\n\nName the remaining variant, or end the chain in `any()`:\n\n```toylang\nenum S { a, b }\n\nS.a | a -> 1 or any() -> 2\n```\n\n```output\n1\n```\n\n## Partial chains and their Opt results\n\nA guard chain with no default is a legitimate way to say \"some inputs don't classify\" --\ndeclining every arm produces `Opt`, not a refusal:\n\n```toylang\nfn discount(total: Int) -> Opt<Int> = total | . >= 100 -> total / 10\n\n{a: discount(150), b: discount(50)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":15,\"b\":null}\n```\n\nThe declared `Opt<T>` return reaches each arm as `T`, the same as any other declared type,\nso a bare `[]` resolves inside a partial arm without needing a variable to infer it from:\n\n```toylang\nfn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> []\n\n{a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n```\n\nConsume the result the way any `Opt` is consumed: `!` if absence should become a runtime\nerror ([unwrap](../reference/operators/unwrap.md)), or fold a default into the chain itself\nif it should not:\n\n```toylang\nfn discount(total: Int) -> Int = total | . >= 100 -> total / 10 or 0\n\n{a: discount(150), b: discount(50)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":15,\"b\":0}\n```\n\nA partial chain whose arms are themselves `Opt`-typed doubles the wrapping: declining the\nchain is one absence, matching and finding nothing is another, and since absence is tagged\nin memory they stay two different values -- `none` and `some(none)`. Note the doubled\nreturn type:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n    entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9]\n\n{\n    a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n    b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [5]})\n}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":null,\"b\":null}\n```\n\nBoth *print* `null`, because serialization flattens every level of tagging away -- it is\nlossy about this the way it is about every type-level distinction. The program can still\ntell them apart: `!` peels exactly one level, so unwrapping `b` yields the inner absence\nand prints `null`, while unwrapping `a` stops the program.\n\nA default arm collapses the doubling back to one level -- even a default that can itself\ncome back absent -- because the chain is no longer partial; every input now hits an arm:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n    entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9]\n\n{\n    a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n    b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n```\n\nThe same chain also runs directly per element inside `map`, guards and field reads over `.`\nwith no named function in between:\n\n```case\nmap_guard_chain_over_vec_field\n```\n\nThe [match reference](../reference/operators/match.md) has the full arm-shape rundown; the\n[matching tutorial chapter](../tutorial/06-matching.md) is the narrative version.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":167,"totalLines":167,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/guides/matching.md","time":{"start":1788300068699,"end":1788300068712}},"id":"prt_05efd84af001LzcL5ljPemP2Ne","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efcab58001Nh9rOWzx0jo9se"}}
