{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300073263,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aec2bf5a384e3204","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/operators/comparison.md","limit":40},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/operators/comparison.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Comparisons\n2: \n3: `==  !=  <  <=  >  >=`, each yielding [Bool](../types/bool.md). Both operands must have the\n4: same type; nothing is coerced.\n5: \n6: ```toylang\n7: [1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2)\n8: ```\n9: \n10: ```output\n11: [2,3]\n12: ```\n13: \n14: Equality works on `Str` as well as `Int`:\n15: \n16: ```toylang\n17: \"ada\" != \"bo\"\n18: ```\n19: \n20: ```output\n21: true\n22: ```\n23: \n24: `==` and `!=` reach inside a composite. Two records are equal when their fields are, two\n25: enum values when they are the same variant carrying equal payloads, and neither asks where\n26: the value came from:\n27: \n28: ```toylang\n29: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n30: \n31: circle({r: 1}) == circle({r: 1})\n32: ```\n33: \n34: ```output\n35: true\n36: ```\n37: \n38: Since [field order is not part of a record type](../types/record.md), two spellings of one\n39: type are one value and compare equal. All of it is pinned on every backend:\n40: \n\n(Showing lines 1-40 of 58. Use offset=41 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Comparisons\n\n`==  !=  <  <=  >  >=`, each yielding [Bool](../types/bool.md). Both operands must have the\nsame type; nothing is coerced.\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2)\n```\n\n```output\n[2,3]\n```\n\nEquality works on `Str` as well as `Int`:\n\n```toylang\n\"ada\" != \"bo\"\n```\n\n```output","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/operators/comparison.md","text":"# Comparisons\n\n`==  !=  <  <=  >  >=`, each yielding [Bool](../types/bool.md). Both operands must have the\nsame type; nothing is coerced.\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2)\n```\n\n```output\n[2,3]\n```\n\nEquality works on `Str` as well as `Int`:\n\n```toylang\n\"ada\" != \"bo\"\n```\n\n```output\ntrue\n```\n\n`==` and `!=` reach inside a composite. Two records are equal when their fields are, two\nenum values when they are the same variant carrying equal payloads, and neither asks where\nthe value came from:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\ncircle({r: 1}) == circle({r: 1})\n```\n\n```output\ntrue\n```\n\nSince [field order is not part of a record type](../types/record.md), two spellings of one\ntype are one value and compare equal. All of it is pinned on every backend:\n","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":40,"totalLines":58,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/reference/operators/comparison.md","time":{"start":1788300073246,"end":1788300073261}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me read the other flagged pages.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05efd9634001panQquN0ZV6MZ4","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efd8792001IYhiVGqZDzBCXT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300073671,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-906ec6a25e19c4b3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/types/enum.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/types/enum.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Enums\n2: \n3: A declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming\n4: one must handle every variant. As data an enum is plain JSON, never an opaque value\n5: ([ADR 0009](../../adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)).\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n9: \n10: {a: Shape.point, b: circle({r: 3})}\n11: ```\n12: \n13: ```output\n14: {\"a\":\"point\",\"b\":{\"circle\":{\"r\":3}}}\n15: ```\n16: \n17: A unit variant carries nothing and is a bare string on the wire. A payload variant carries\n18: one type and is the single-key wrapper. The payload can be any single type: a record\n19: declared in braces (`circle{r: Int}`), or anything else in parens the way a call passes a\n20: non-record argument:\n21: \n22: ```case\n23: enum_scalar_payload\n24: ```\n25: \n26: A variant name is data, so it is lowercase, like a field. Construction is ordinary\n27: application of the constructor the declaration derives -- `circle{r: 3}`, `celsius(21)` --\n28: and the bare unit-variant name works while exactly one enum claims it; `Shape.point` is the\n29: qualified way out when two do.\n30: \n31: Consumption is the [match](../operators/match.md), which is closed-world: every variant\n32: handled, or an `any()` arm for the rest. A program whose match misses a variant is refused:\n33: \n34: ```toylang\n35: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n36: \n37: fn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r\n38: \n39: area_ish(Shape.point)\n40: ```\n41: \n42: ```error\n43: a match over `Shape` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `point` (at byte 73)\n44: ```\n45: \n46: Because the wire shape is plain JSON, an enum types input directly, and the input is\n47: validated against the declared set: a string that names no variant, or a wrapper whose\n48: payload misses the declared type, is refused before the program runs.\n49: \n50: ```case\n51: enum_input\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ## An enum that contains itself\n55: \n56: A payload may name the enum being declared, as long as it does so through a `Vec`. That is\n57: what makes JSON's own shape expressible: an array case holds a `Vec` of the same type, which\n58: is a heap indirection rather than a value with no end.\n59: \n60: ```case\n61: enum_recursive_value\n62: ```\n63: \n64: The rule is per occurrence, not per declaration, so `enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }` accepts\n65: `safe` and still refuses `bad`: a bare self-reference is a layout that contains itself.\n66: \n67: ```toylang\n68: enum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int), node{next: Json} }\n69: \n70: Json.num(1)\n71: ```\n72: \n73: ```error\n74: type `Json` is written in terms of itself (at byte 49)\n75: ```\n\n(End of file - total 75 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Enums\n\nA declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming\none must handle every variant. As data an enum is plain JSON, never an opaque value\n([ADR 0009](../../adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)).\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\n{a: Shape.point, b: circle({r: 3})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"point\",\"b\":{\"circle\":{\"r\":3}}}\n```\n\nA unit variant carries nothing and is a bare string on the wire. A payload variant carries\none type and is the single-key wrapper. The payload can be any single type: a record\ndeclared in braces (`circle{r: Int}`), or anything else in parens the way a call passes a\nnon-record argument:","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/reference/types/enum.md","text":"# Enums\n\nA declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming\none must handle every variant. As data an enum is plain JSON, never an opaque value\n([ADR 0009](../../adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)).\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\n{a: Shape.point, b: circle({r: 3})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":\"point\",\"b\":{\"circle\":{\"r\":3}}}\n```\n\nA unit variant carries nothing and is a bare string on the wire. A payload variant carries\none type and is the single-key wrapper. The payload can be any single type: a record\ndeclared in braces (`circle{r: Int}`), or anything else in parens the way a call passes a\nnon-record argument:\n\n```case\nenum_scalar_payload\n```\n\nA variant name is data, so it is lowercase, like a field. Construction is ordinary\napplication of the constructor the declaration derives -- `circle{r: 3}`, `celsius(21)` --\nand the bare unit-variant name works while exactly one enum claims it; `Shape.point` is the\nqualified way out when two do.\n\nConsumption is the [match](../operators/match.md), which is closed-world: every variant\nhandled, or an `any()` arm for the rest. A program whose match misses a variant is refused:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\nfn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r\n\narea_ish(Shape.point)\n```\n\n```error\na match over `Shape` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `point` (at byte 73)\n```\n\nBecause the wire shape is plain JSON, an enum types input directly, and the input is\nvalidated against the declared set: a string that names no variant, or a wrapper whose\npayload misses the declared type, is refused before the program runs.\n\n```case\nenum_input\n```\n\n## An enum that contains itself\n\nA payload may name the enum being declared, as long as it does so through a `Vec`. That is\nwhat makes JSON's own shape expressible: an array case holds a `Vec` of the same type, which\nis a heap indirection rather than a value with no end.\n\n```case\nenum_recursive_value\n```\n\nThe rule is per occurrence, not per declaration, so `enum E { safe(Vec<E>), bad(E) }` accepts\n`safe` and still refuses `bad`: a bare self-reference is a layout that contains itself.\n\n```toylang\nenum Json { arr(Vec<Json>), num(Int), node{next: Json} }\n\nJson.num(1)\n```\n\n```error\ntype `Json` is written in terms of itself (at byte 49)\n```","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":75,"totalLines":75,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/types/enum.md","time":{"start":1788300073664,"end":1788300073668}},"id":"prt_05efd9923001ZXkPH40JVK44zA","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efd8792001IYhiVGqZDzBCXT"}}
