{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788300075104,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05efda059001iqweFVvtcJBWSf","messageID":"msg_05efd9ae8001tXiRyt00IoPzew","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","snapshot":"d0271ce2cd9455202635e4e404ebf88e5b506c70","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300075429,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8de6af13acb79acf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/04-enums.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/04-enums.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Enums\n2: \n3: Some data is one of a known set of shapes: a status is `active` or `inactive`, a shape is a\n4: point or a circle with a radius. An enum declares the set, closed:\n5: \n6: ```toylang\n7: enum Status { active, inactive }\n8: \n9: Status.active\n10: ```\n11: \n12: ```output\n13: \"active\"\n14: ```\n15: \n16: Note what printed: a bare string. An enum is plain JSON on the wire -- a unit variant is a\n17: string, a payload variant is a single-key wrapper -- so enums type real wire data directly\n18: rather than inventing a private encoding.\n19: \n20: ```toylang\n21: # fmt: syntax-example\n22: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n23: \n24: circle{r: 3}\n25: ```\n26: \n27: ```output\n28: {\"circle\":{\"r\":3}}\n29: ```\n30: \n31: Constructing a variant is ordinary application of the constructor the declaration derives.\n32: The bare name (`circle{r: 3}`, `active`) works while only one enum claims it; the qualified\n33: `Shape.point` always works.\n34: \n35: ## Match\n36: \n37: Consuming an enum must handle every variant. The subject arrives through a pipe, arms chain\n38: with `or`, first match wins:\n39: \n40: ```toylang\n41: enum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n42: \n43: fn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point -> 0\n44: \n45: {a: area_ish(Shape.point), b: area_ish(circle({r: 3}))}\n46: ```\n47: \n48: ```output\n49: {\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n50: ```\n51: \n52: `circle{r}` binds the payload's field; a unit arm is just the name. Leave a variant out and\n53: the program is refused at compile time, naming what is missing -- unless the chain ends in\n54: `any()`, the explicit way to say \"everything else\":\n55: \n56: ```case\n57: enum_match_default\n58: ```\n59: \n60: The payload can be any single type, not only a record -- `celsius(Int)`, `some(Vec<Int>)` --\n61: and in a bare payload arm `.` becomes the payload itself. The\n62: [guide on enums](../guides/enums.md) works through typing a real wire format with these.\n63: \n64: A match arm can also be a guard -- a Bool test instead of a variant pattern -- which is\n65: [matching](06-matching.md)'s subject, once streams (next) round out the pipeline shapes worth\n66: branching on.\n67: \n68: Next: [streams](05-streams.md), where the data comes from stdin.\n\n(End of file - total 68 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Enums\n\nSome data is one of a known set of shapes: a status is `active` or `inactive`, a shape is a\npoint or a circle with a radius. An enum declares the set, closed:\n\n```toylang\nenum Status { active, inactive }\n\nStatus.active\n```\n\n```output\n\"active\"\n```\n\nNote what printed: a bare string. An enum is plain JSON on the wire -- a unit variant is a\nstring, a payload variant is a single-key wrapper -- so enums type real wire data directly\nrather than inventing a private encoding.\n\n```toylang","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/04-enums.md","text":"# Enums\n\nSome data is one of a known set of shapes: a status is `active` or `inactive`, a shape is a\npoint or a circle with a radius. An enum declares the set, closed:\n\n```toylang\nenum Status { active, inactive }\n\nStatus.active\n```\n\n```output\n\"active\"\n```\n\nNote what printed: a bare string. An enum is plain JSON on the wire -- a unit variant is a\nstring, a payload variant is a single-key wrapper -- so enums type real wire data directly\nrather than inventing a private encoding.\n\n```toylang\n# fmt: syntax-example\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\ncircle{r: 3}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"circle\":{\"r\":3}}\n```\n\nConstructing a variant is ordinary application of the constructor the declaration derives.\nThe bare name (`circle{r: 3}`, `active`) works while only one enum claims it; the qualified\n`Shape.point` always works.\n\n## Match\n\nConsuming an enum must handle every variant. The subject arrives through a pipe, arms chain\nwith `or`, first match wins:\n\n```toylang\nenum Shape { point, circle{r: Int} }\n\nfn area_ish(s: Shape) -> Int = s | circle{r} -> r * r or point -> 0\n\n{a: area_ish(Shape.point), b: area_ish(circle({r: 3}))}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":0,\"b\":9}\n```\n\n`circle{r}` binds the payload's field; a unit arm is just the name. Leave a variant out and\nthe program is refused at compile time, naming what is missing -- unless the chain ends in\n`any()`, the explicit way to say \"everything else\":\n\n```case\nenum_match_default\n```\n\nThe payload can be any single type, not only a record -- `celsius(Int)`, `some(Vec<Int>)` --\nand in a bare payload arm `.` becomes the payload itself. The\n[guide on enums](../guides/enums.md) works through typing a real wire format with these.\n\nA match arm can also be a guard -- a Bool test instead of a variant pattern -- which is\n[matching](06-matching.md)'s subject, once streams (next) round out the pipeline shapes worth\nbranching on.\n\nNext: [streams](05-streams.md), where the data comes from stdin.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":68,"totalLines":68,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md","time":{"start":1788300075411,"end":1788300075427}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_05efda05e001ChvhKJWpMAsg25","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efd9ae8001tXiRyt00IoPzew"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300076162,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-801b47120597ee38","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","limit":80,"offset":55},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n55: to its own result, and the chain's final bare expression is the default:\n56: \n57: ```case\n58: fizzbuzz_arms\n59: ```\n60: \n61: ## Hybrid totality\n62: \n63: A variant match is closed-world: chapter 4 showed that leaving a variant out is refused,\n64: naming it, unless the chain ends in `any()`. A guard is a runtime Bool the checker cannot see\n65: through, so no guard -- however certain -- contributes to that coverage:\n66: \n67: ```toylang\n68: enum S { a, b }\n69: \n70: S.a | a -> 1 or 1 == 1 -> 2\n71: ```\n72: \n73: ```error\n74: a match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b` (at byte 23)\n75: ```\n76: \n77: `1 == 1` always matches at runtime, but the checker only counts named variants and `any()`.\n78: Naming `b` or adding `any()` are the only two ways to close this chain.\n79: \n80: ## Partial guard chains and Opt\n81: \n82: A pure guard chain -- no variant patterns, just guards -- may skip the default entirely.\n83: Declining every arm is not a compile error: it is the same `Opt` an out-of-range index\n84: already gives, printed as `null`:\n85: \n86: ```case\n87: match_partial_guards\n88: ```\n89: \n90: A declared `Opt<T>` return reaches the arms as `T`, not `Opt<T>` -- the chain's own\n91: partiality is what supplies the `Opt`, so an arm that writes a bare `[]` resolves it against\n92: the declared element type instead of needing it spelled out some other way:\n93: \n94: ```toylang\n95: fn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> []\n96: \n97: {a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n98: ```\n99: \n100: ```output\n101: {\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n102: ```\n103: \n104: When a partial chain's arms are themselves `Opt`-typed, the wrapping doubles: \"no arm\n105: matched\" and \"matched, and found nothing\" are two different values in memory (`none` and\n106: `some(none)`), even though both print `null` once serialization flattens the tags away.\n107: The return type says so honestly:\n108: \n109: ```toylang\n110: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n111:     entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9]\n112: \n113: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]})\n114: ```\n115: \n116: ```output\n117: null\n118: ```\n119: \n120: A default arm collapses the doubling, because the chain is no longer partial:\n121: \n122: ```toylang\n123: fn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n124:     entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9]\n125: \n126: {\n127:     a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n128:     b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n129: }\n130: ```\n131: \n132: ```output\n133: {\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n134: ```\n\n(Showing lines 55-134 of 140. Use offset=135 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"to its own result, and the chain's final bare expression is the default:\n\n```case\nfizzbuzz_arms\n```\n\n## Hybrid totality\n\nA variant match is closed-world: chapter 4 showed that leaving a variant out is refused,\nnaming it, unless the chain ends in `any()`. A guard is a runtime Bool the checker cannot see\nthrough, so no guard -- however certain -- contributes to that coverage:\n\n```toylang\nenum S { a, b }\n\nS.a | a -> 1 or 1 == 1 -> 2\n```\n\n```error\na match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b` (at byte 23)","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","text":"to its own result, and the chain's final bare expression is the default:\n\n```case\nfizzbuzz_arms\n```\n\n## Hybrid totality\n\nA variant match is closed-world: chapter 4 showed that leaving a variant out is refused,\nnaming it, unless the chain ends in `any()`. A guard is a runtime Bool the checker cannot see\nthrough, so no guard -- however certain -- contributes to that coverage:\n\n```toylang\nenum S { a, b }\n\nS.a | a -> 1 or 1 == 1 -> 2\n```\n\n```error\na match over `S` must cover every variant or end in a default; missing `b` (at byte 23)\n```\n\n`1 == 1` always matches at runtime, but the checker only counts named variants and `any()`.\nNaming `b` or adding `any()` are the only two ways to close this chain.\n\n## Partial guard chains and Opt\n\nA pure guard chain -- no variant patterns, just guards -- may skip the default entirely.\nDeclining every arm is not a compile error: it is the same `Opt` an out-of-range index\nalready gives, printed as `null`:\n\n```case\nmatch_partial_guards\n```\n\nA declared `Opt<T>` return reaches the arms as `T`, not `Opt<T>` -- the chain's own\npartiality is what supplies the `Opt`, so an arm that writes a bare `[]` resolves it against\nthe declared element type instead of needing it spelled out some other way:\n\n```toylang\nfn tags(n: Int) -> Opt<Vec<Int>> = n | . > 0 -> []\n\n{a: tags(1), b: tags(-1)}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":[],\"b\":null}\n```\n\nWhen a partial chain's arms are themselves `Opt`-typed, the wrapping doubles: \"no arm\nmatched\" and \"matched, and found nothing\" are two different values in memory (`none` and\n`some(none)`), even though both print `null` once serialization flattens the tags away.\nThe return type says so honestly:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Opt<Int>> =\n    entry | .valid -> entry.readings[9]\n\nfirst_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]})\n```\n\n```output\nnull\n```\n\nA default arm collapses the doubling, because the chain is no longer partial:\n\n```toylang\nfn first_reading(entry: {valid: Bool, readings: Vec<Int>}) -> Opt<Int> =\n    entry | .valid -> entry.readings[0] or entry.readings[9]\n\n{\n    a: first_reading({valid: 1 == 2, readings: [5]}),\n    b: first_reading({valid: 1 == 1, readings: [1]})\n}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"a\":null,\"b\":1}\n```","lineStart":55,"lineEnd":134,"totalLines":140,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md","time":{"start":1788300076153,"end":1788300076158}},"id":"prt_05efda199001OHnJ3WW0DNtkiM","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05efd9ae8001tXiRyt00IoPzew"}}
