{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788300253427,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05f0058e9001YlvX0Fk7fjDZ41","messageID":"msg_05f005137001JJMz1nku5nknye","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","snapshot":"d0271ce2cd9455202635e4e404ebf88e5b506c70","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300338413,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a262121c0ed92bb5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/clippy.toml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/clippy.toml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Budgets for the clippy lints in Cargo.toml, which clippy reads for itself.\n2: #\n3: # They are not in .claude/checks/limits.toml with the file budget because\n4: # clippy owns both the measurement and the comparison; the check runner only\n5: # forwards what it says, and a finding carries the number with it, so there is\n6: # still one place to read each budget from.\n7: #\n8: # All three ratchet down by deliberate commits, never up to make a finding go\n9: # away. The targets are toy-browser's numbers: 5 / 40 / 1. The starts are set\n10: # where this tree's standing findings are countable rather than wallpaper; at\n11: # the targets there would be 82 on day one, and a check that fires constantly\n12: # is a check that stops being read. See plans/quality-practices.md.\n13: \n14: # 10 catches ten functions today, all in emitters and parse.rs. One warning\n15: # about the metric: clippy counts if/else/loops/guards but not match arms, and\n16: # the emitters are mostly wide matches over TIR, so the score ranks nothing.\n17: # Look at the shape, not the number.\n18: cognitive-complexity-threshold = 10\n19: \n20: # How long one function's body may be, counting neither comments nor blank\n21: # lines, so it measures code where `max_file_lines` deliberately measures\n22: # prose as well. It exists because a long straight function is invisible to\n23: # both the complexity and the file budget. 100 catches 15 functions today,\n24: # the worst being check.rs's 445-line checker body.\n25: too-many-lines-threshold = 100\n26: \n27: # Two bools in a signature usually means two callers sharing one body, which\n28: # is the cause `cognitive-complexity` only ever sees as a symptom. At 1 this\n29: # fires exactly once today (run_jq in src/lib.rs), and that once is worth a\n30: # look.\n31: max-fn-params-bools = 1\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Budgets for the clippy lints in Cargo.toml, which clippy reads for itself.\n#\n# They are not in .claude/checks/limits.toml with the file budget because\n# clippy owns both the measurement and the comparison; the check runner only\n# forwards what it says, and a finding carries the number with it, so there is\n# still one place to read each budget from.\n#\n# All three ratchet down by deliberate commits, never up to make a finding go\n# away. The targets are toy-browser's numbers: 5 / 40 / 1. The starts are set\n# where this tree's standing findings are countable rather than wallpaper; at\n# the targets there would be 82 on day one, and a check that fires constantly\n# is a check that stops being read. See plans/quality-practices.md.\n\n# 10 catches ten functions today, all in emitters and parse.rs. One warning\n# about the metric: clippy counts if/else/loops/guards but not match arms, and\n# the emitters are mostly wide matches over TIR, so the score ranks nothing.\n# Look at the shape, not the number.\ncognitive-complexity-threshold = 10\n\n# How long one function's body may be, counting neither comments nor blank","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/clippy.toml","text":"# Budgets for the clippy lints in Cargo.toml, which clippy reads for itself.\n#\n# They are not in .claude/checks/limits.toml with the file budget because\n# clippy owns both the measurement and the comparison; the check runner only\n# forwards what it says, and a finding carries the number with it, so there is\n# still one place to read each budget from.\n#\n# All three ratchet down by deliberate commits, never up to make a finding go\n# away. The targets are toy-browser's numbers: 5 / 40 / 1. The starts are set\n# where this tree's standing findings are countable rather than wallpaper; at\n# the targets there would be 82 on day one, and a check that fires constantly\n# is a check that stops being read. See plans/quality-practices.md.\n\n# 10 catches ten functions today, all in emitters and parse.rs. One warning\n# about the metric: clippy counts if/else/loops/guards but not match arms, and\n# the emitters are mostly wide matches over TIR, so the score ranks nothing.\n# Look at the shape, not the number.\ncognitive-complexity-threshold = 10\n\n# How long one function's body may be, counting neither comments nor blank\n# lines, so it measures code where `max_file_lines` deliberately measures\n# prose as well. It exists because a long straight function is invisible to\n# both the complexity and the file budget. 100 catches 15 functions today,\n# the worst being check.rs's 445-line checker body.\ntoo-many-lines-threshold = 100\n\n# Two bools in a signature usually means two callers sharing one body, which\n# is the cause `cognitive-complexity` only ever sees as a symptom. At 1 this\n# fires exactly once today (run_jq in src/lib.rs), and that once is worth a\n# look.\nmax-fn-params-bools = 1","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"clippy.toml","time":{"start":1788300338398,"end":1788300338411}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Confirmed. The failure set matches LAND-FAILURE.txt exactly: 7 BROKEN fragments and 4 MISQUOTED fragments. Let me capture the full list of unique `at` locations:\n\nBROKEN:\n- docs/guides/enums.md:13\n- docs/guides/matching.md:66\n- docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28\n- docs/reference/types/enum.md:7\n- docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6\n- docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20\n- docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40\n\nMISQUOTED:\n- docs/guides/matching.md:54\n- docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\n- docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\n- docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\n\nNow let me design the gate. I'll add a gating mechanism in tests/docs.rs.\n\nDesign decision: The gate should:\n1. Match exactly the flagged locations (page + opening-fence line).\n2. For gated fragments, replace the normal expectation check with a weak but real check: assert the program still fails to compile (it is rejected by the checker, pending the doc migration). This keeps the gate honest — it's not a silent skip; it pins the current broken state. When the migration lands and the gate is removed, the full expectation returns.\n3. Leave a comment at the gate pointing to the `doc-migrate-capital-variants` board row.\n\nWait — let me reconsider whether \"assert it fails to compile\" is the right weak check for both BROKEN and MISQUOTED.\n\n- BROKEN: fragments with `output` or `refuses` fences that now fail at compile. The weak check \"still fails to compile\" matches the current state. Good.\n- MISQUOTED: fragments with `error` fences where the checker message differs from the page. The weak check \"still fails to compile\" (regardless of message) matches the current state — the program IS rejected by the checker (just with a different message than the page claims). Good.\n\nBut here's a subtlety: for the MISQUOTED cases, after migration these programs would still be `error` fragments but with a different expected message (the page claims a coverage error / self-reference error, which are real errors the program would hit once it compiles past casing). So post-migration, the gate would be removed and the error check returns with the page's claimed message. If the page's message matches the checker's post-migration message, green; if not, MISQUOTED again — which the migration row would fix. Fine.\n\nNow, one important design question: should the weak check assert \"fails to compile\" as a tripwire, or should it be a pure skip? I think asserting \"fails to compile\" is better per the repo philosophy (not a silent skip, and it trips if the fragment starts compiling before the gate is removed). The AGENTS.md test section says \"Don't assert things that cannot fail.\" Asserting \"fails to compile\" CAN fail (if the migration happens without removing the gate, or if someone fixes one fragment), so it's a legitimate assertion.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a nuance. If the weak check asserts \"fails to compile\", and one day a fragment gets migrated in the doc content but the gate isn't removed, the test would go red with a confusing message like \"docs/guides/enums.md:13: expected to still be refused until the doc-migrate-capital-variants migration, but it compiles\". That's actually good — it signals the gate is stale.\n\nLet me write a helper that returns the gated-fragment behavior. Let me structure it as:\n\n```rust\n/// The doc pages still spell lowercase-leading variants the gh:156 casing checker now refuses.\n/// Gate these fragments to the checker's current refusal instead of their (stale) expectation\n/// until the doc-migrate-capital-variants board row migrates the content and removes this list.\n/// A fragment that starts compiling before then trips the gate, so it cannot rot silently.\nfn capital_variant_gate(at: &str) -> bool {\n    matches!(\n        at,\n        \"docs/guides/enums.md:13\"\n            | \"docs/guides/matching.md:54\"\n            | \"docs/guides/matching.md:66\"\n            | \"docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:7\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\"\n    )\n}\n\n/// The gated check: the program must still be refused by the checker, whichever way it is\n/// refused. That pins the fragment as pending the capital-variant migration without asserting\n/// the stale expectation fence it currently cannot meet.\nfn gated_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n        Ok(_) => vec![format!(\n            \"GATED {}: compiles, but it is gated on the doc-migrate-capital-variants migration; remove the gate and restore the expectation fence\",\n            f.at\n        )],\n        Err(_) => Vec::new(),\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen in the main loop:\n\n```rust\nfor f in &fragments {\n    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n        failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n        continue;\n    }\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n        ...\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWait — the gating check placement relative to slow. None of the gated fragments are slow, so placement before or after the slow check doesn't matter, but logically the gate should be checked first (a gated fragment bypasses everything). Actually let me put it right after the slow check, or before. Since the gate is about the capital-variant migration, and slow is about execution cost, I'll put the gate check first so it short-circuits. Hmm, but actually if a gated fragment were also slow, we'd want... there are none. Put gate first.\n\nNow, should I also think about whether the module docstring at the top of docs.rs needs updating? The docstring says \"There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that.\" (line 13). This gate IS a way to opt out, so the docstring would now be inaccurate. I should update it to mention the temporary capital-variant gate. The AGENTS.md says docstring length should track surprise. I'll add a brief note.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a gitignored path...\" — this paragraph explains why fixture was removed. Adding a one-time exception with a visible gate comment... The gate is an exception to \"no way to opt out\". I should mention it in the module docstring so the docs don't lie. Let me add a sentence.\n\nLet me think about the exact wording. The module doc says the harness exists so \"the documentation cannot lie\". The gate means a handful of fragments currently can't be verified. I'll add a short paragraph.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is asserting \"fails to compile\" the best weak check, or should the gate assert the specific checker message (the variant-casing message)? The brief said \"6 doc pages still declare lowercase-leading variants the checker now rejects, plus 4 fixtures assert stale error text (MISQUOTED)\". The exact error strings are in the board row (which I don't have verbatim on this branch, but LAND-FAILURE.txt has them). Asserting the specific message would be more precise but brittle — actually it would be MORE meaningful: it pins that the checker still rejects for the casing reason. But post-migration, that message disappears, tripping the gate — good too. However, the MISQUOTED fixtures currently produce a *casing* error message that doesn't match their page; post-migration they'll hit their page's described error. For the gate to be a clean tripwire, \"fails to compile\" (any reason) is simpler and matches \"the checker now rejects\". I'll go with \"must still fail to compile\".\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. There's a risk with \"fails to compile\": these fragments will fail to compile for the same casing reason forever until migration. That's fine. But could the checker change such that a gated fragment starts passing the casing check but still fails for another reason? Then the gate still passes (still fails to compile) but the gate is semantically stale (should be removed). That's acceptable — the migration row handles removal.\n\nActually, the stronger check (assert the casing message specifically) has a real advantage: it guarantees the gate only masks the *casing* failure, not any other kind of breakage. E.g. if the fragment's program had a syntax error introduced by a future edit, \"fails to compile\" would still pass and silently mask it. Whereas asserting the casing message would trip. Hmm. But the exact message includes `(at byte N)` which is fragile. I could assert the message starts with \"a variant name starts with a capital letter\". That's precise enough to ensure the gate only masks the casing rejection, and trips on any other failure mode.\n\nLet me go with: for gated fragments, compile must fail with a message starting with \"a variant name starts with a capital letter\". This is the strongest honest check: it pins the exact reason these fragments are gated (the casing rule), and any other failure mode (syntax error, or the program compiling) trips the gate.\n\nWait, but is that right for all 11? Let me verify:\n- All 7 BROKEN: \"a variant name starts with a capital letter; ...\" — yes.\n- All 4 MISQUOTED: the checker says \"a variant name starts with a capital letter; ...\" — yes (from LAND-FAILURE.txt).\n\nGreat, all 11 currently fail with the casing message. So the gate check: `Err(e) if e.to_string().starts_with(\"a variant name starts with a capital letter\")` passes; anything else (Ok, or a different error) fails the gate.\n\nThis is the best design:\n1. Not a silent skip — runs the compiler, asserts a precise claim.\n2. Only masks the specific casing failure the migration will fix.\n3. Trips if a gated fragment starts compiling OR fails for any other reason.\n4. Comment points to doc-migrate-capital-variants.\n\nNow let me also double check the exact prefix string. From src/check/types.rs:109: \"a variant name starts with a capital letter; declare ...\". So the prefix \"a variant name starts with a capital letter\" is exact.\n\nLet me write a helper `casing_gate` and a `gated_failures` function.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the brief says \"gate or xfail exactly the flagged cases\". My design does exactly that.\n\nNow, let me write the code. I'll edit tests/docs.rs.\n\nFirst, add the module docstring note. Then add the helper functions and the gate in the loop.\n\nLet me draft the edits.\n\nEdit 1: module docstring. Current lines 13-18:\n```\n//! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n//! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n//! was absent, which it was for everyone; the four pages using it published an answer nobody\n//! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n//! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n//! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n```\n\nI'll add a sentence after this paragraph noting the one deliberate temporary exception: the capital-variant gate. Something like:\n\n```\n//!\n//! The one deliberate exception is the gh:156 capital-variant gate: the doc pages still spell\n//! variant names lowercase, which the casing checker now refuses, so `every_fragment_is_a_real_program`\n//! gates those fragments to the checker's refusal instead of their stale expectation. It is not a\n//! skip -- the program must still be refused for the casing reason, or the gate trips -- and\n//! doc-migrate-capital-variants removes it once the content is migrated.\n```\n\nHmm, wait — module docstrings here use `//!`. Let me make sure it reads well and is accurate.\n\nEdit 2: In the loop, add the gate before the outcome match.\n\nEdit 3: Add helper functions near compile_only_failures.\n\nLet me write these edits. Let me look at exact line content to edit precisely.\n\nCurrent loop (lines 92-131):\n```rust\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n```\n\nI'll insert the gate check before the slow check:\n```rust\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n            failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n```\n\nNow the helpers. I'll place `gated_failures` and `capital_variant_gate` after `compile_only_failures` (which ends at line 175) and before `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page`.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```rust\n/// The fragments still spelling lowercase variant names the gh:156 casing checker now refuses.\n/// These are gated to the checker's current refusal until the doc-migrate-capital-variants board\n/// row migrates the content and removes this list, so the checker stays enforced while `just\n/// test` goes green. They are not skipped: the program must still be refused for the casing\n/// reason, so a fragment that compiles, or fails for any other cause, trips the gate instead of\n/// rotting.\nfn capital_variant_gate(at: &str) -> bool {\n    matches!(\n        at,\n        \"docs/guides/enums.md:13\"\n            | \"docs/guides/matching.md:54\"\n            | \"docs/guides/matching.md:66\"\n            | \"docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:7\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\"\n            | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40\"\n            | \"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\"\n    )\n}\n\n/// The gated check: the program must still be refused for the capital-variant casing rule,\n/// whichever of `output`, `refuses`, or `error` the fragment declared it wanted. That pins the\n/// fragment as pending the doc-migrate-capital-variants migration without asserting the stale\n/// expectation fence it currently cannot meet.\nfn gated_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n        Ok(_) => vec![format!(\n            \"GATED {}: compiles, but it is gated on the doc-migrate-capital-variants migration; remove the gate and restore the expectation fence\",\n            f.at\n        )],\n        Err(e) if e.to_string().starts_with(\"a variant name starts with a capital letter\") => {\n            Vec::new()\n        }\n        Err(e) => vec![format!(\n            \"GATED {}: refused, but not for the capital-variant casing rule the gate exists for: {e}\",\n            f.at\n        )],\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait — AGENTS.md says comments: \"Comment the why. The what is already there in the code\". The docstrings I wrote are explaining why. Good. But note \"Don't announce compliance\" and \"Comment the why\". The docstring is longish but it's for a genuinely non-obvious thing (a gate in a harness that explicitly forbids skipping). It deserves explanation.\n\nAlso, I should double-check that clippy won't complain about the long format string line. Let me check the line width budget — there's a clippy.toml. Let me check it.\n\nLet me look at clippy.toml for the line-width setting.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f01a1d5001ZBapGuRMnuNmGY","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f005137001JJMz1nku5nknye"}}
