{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300385028,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9f570f37f80901c2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs","limit":70,"offset":160},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n160: /// Whether the slow tier executes. `TOYLANG_SLOW=1` turns it on; the default run compiles a\n161: /// `slow` fragment on every backend without running it.\n162: fn slow_runs_enabled() -> bool {\n163:     std::env::var(\"TOYLANG_SLOW\").is_ok_and(|v| v == \"1\")\n164: }\n165: \n166: /// The default check for a `slow` fragment. Running it is what costs minutes, so the default\n167: /// run proves only what is cheap: it parses, type-checks, and emits on every backend. The\n168: /// agreement and the printed answer are the opt-in run's claim.\n169: fn compile_only_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n170:     let program = match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n171:         Ok(p) => p,\n172:         Err(e) => return vec![format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at)],\n173:     };\n174:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n175:     for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n176:         if let Err(e) = backend.emit(&program) {\n177:             failures.push(format!(\n178:                 \"BROKEN  {}: {} could not emit: {e}\",\n179:                 f.at,\n180:                 backend.name()\n181:             ));\n182:         }\n183:     }\n184:     failures\n185: }\n186: \n187: /// The fragments still spelling lowercase variant names the gh:156 casing checker now refuses.\n188: /// These are gated to the checker's current refusal until the doc-migrate-capital-variants board\n189: /// row migrates the content and removes this list, so the checker stays enforced while `just\n190: /// test` goes green. The list is page:line of the opening `toylang` fence, exactly the `at` a\n191: /// failure names.\n192: fn capital_variant_gate(at: &str) -> bool {\n193:     matches!(\n194:         at,\n195:         \"docs/guides/enums.md:13\"\n196:             | \"docs/guides/matching.md:54\"\n197:             | \"docs/guides/matching.md:66\"\n198:             | \"docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28\"\n199:             | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:7\"\n200:             | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:34\"\n201:             | \"docs/reference/types/enum.md:67\"\n202:             | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6\"\n203:             | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:20\"\n204:             | \"docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:40\"\n205:             | \"docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67\"\n206:     )\n207: }\n208: \n209: /// The gated check: the program must still be refused for the capital-variant casing rule,\n210: /// whichever of `output`, `refuses`, or `error` the fragment declared it wanted. That pins the\n211: /// fragment as pending the migration without asserting the stale expectation fence it cannot\n212: /// currently meet, and it means the gate cannot silently cover a broken fragment: compiling, or\n213: /// failing for any other reason, trips it.\n214: fn gated_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n215:     match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n216:         Ok(_) => vec![format!(\n217:             \"GATED {}: compiles, but it is gated on the doc-migrate-capital-variants migration; \\\n218:              remove the gate and restore the expectation fence\",\n219:             f.at\n220:         )],\n221:         Err(e) if e.to_string().starts_with(\"a variant name starts with a capital letter\") => {\n222:             Vec::new()\n223:         }\n224:         Err(e) => vec![format!(\n225:             \"GATED {}: refused, but not for the capital-variant casing rule the gate exists for: {e}\",\n226:             f.at\n227:         )],\n228:     }\n229: }\n\n(Showing lines 160-229 of 379. Use offset=230 to continue.)