code you touched\". But there might be standing warnings already. Let me check whether this warning existed before my change.\n\nThe `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` function — I added 4 lines (the gate check block) inside the loop, which adds to cognitive complexity. Let me check if this warning was pre-existing. Let me stash my change and run clippy... but I can't easily stash without being careful. Actually let me check git to see if clippy is currently clean on the base.\n\nActually, let me think: the loop `for f in &fragments { if gate { continue } if slow && !slow_runs_enabled() { ... } match ... }`. The gate added an `if ... continue` inside the loop, which is one more branch counted in cognitive complexity. The original function may have already been at or near the threshold.\n\nLet me check: was there a warning before my change? Let me use git stash to check the baseline clippy on tests/docs.rs. Actually, a cleaner way: check `git show HEAD:tests/docs.rs` — no, that doesn't run clippy.\n\nLet me just temporarily check: `git stash` then `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` then `git stash pop`. But stashing is a git operation that modifies working tree — allowed? The brief says work only inside this worktree, no push, don't touch plans/. Stashing is fine but risky if something goes wrong. Alternatively, I can check the complexity of the original function by reading the original code — the original had:\n\n```\nfor f in &fragments {\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n        failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n        continue;\n    }\n    match &f.outcome {\n        Outcome::Output(want) => ...,\n        Outcome::Refusal => {\n            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                failures.push(...);\n            } else {\n                failures.extend(agreement_failures(...));\n            }\n        }\n        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n            Ok(_) => ...,\n            Err(e) if ... => ...,\n            Err(_) => {},\n        },\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCognitive complexity counts: for loop (+1?), if/else, match arms (match doesn't count arms but counts the match itself +1), if-let. The original was probably already at 10+ and maybe just under. Adding `if capital_variant_gate { ...; continue; }` adds +1 for the if (and maybe +1 for continue? no, continue isn't counted).\n\nHmm, the question is whether clippy was \"clean\" before my change. Let me check by running clippy on the parent commit state of just this file... Actually simplest: stash my changes, run clippy, restore. Let me be careful with stash pop.\n\nActually, an even safer approach: check the git blame/history — was this warning recently added? Let me look at whether the pre-change file triggers the same warning by checking with `git stash`. I'll do it carefully.\n\nWait — actually, there's a subtlety. The `just clippy` output shows the warning for `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` at 14/10. If the baseline was already 14/10 (pre-existing), then it's not something I introduced and \"clean on code you touched\" might already have had this standing warning. But the more likely story: my 4-line addition pushed it over. Let me verify.\n\nLet me stash and check.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f06264c001IXqc66ti2ysrwK","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f05d967001KgVF80VF1M7xUA"}}
