{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300731809,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9bfdb6da50f5da72","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git stash && cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets 2>&1 | rg -n -B1 -A3 \"docs.rs:2[0-9][0-9]:\" ; git stash pop","timeout":300000},"output":"Saved working directory and index state WIP on issue-156: 3b36d15 Correct the provenance on the origin/main merge (fe74bea)\nOn branch issue-156\nChanges not staged for commit:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\n\tmodified:   tests/docs.rs\n\nUntracked files:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\n\tLAND-FAILURE.txt\n\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\nDropped refs/stash@{0} (f8199cd822c2ebaf0324b7921a96f21080143eaf)\n","metadata":{"output":"Saved working directory and index state WIP on issue-156: 3b36d15 Correct the provenance on the origin/main merge (fe74bea)\nOn branch issue-156\nChanges not staged for commit:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\n\tmodified:   tests/docs.rs\n\nUntracked files:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\n\tLAND-FAILURE.txt\n\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\nDropped refs/stash@{0} (f8199cd822c2ebaf0324b7921a96f21080143eaf)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git stash && cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets 2>&1 | rg -n -B1 -A3 \"docs.rs:2[0-9][0-9]:\" ; git stash pop","time":{"start":1788300731804,"end":1788300731807}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The baseline already has the warning at 13/10 (pre-existing standing warning). My change pushed it to 14/10. Since the baseline already warns, \"just clippy clean on code you touched\" can't mean \"zero clippy warnings\" (the repo has dozens standing). But my change did make this specific function worse (13 -> 14). To be a good citizen and not add to the count, I should keep the complexity from increasing.\n\nCan I refactor to avoid adding complexity? The gate adds `if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) { failures.extend(gated_failures(f)); continue; }` — one `if` inside a loop = +1 cognitive complexity (if counts as +1). Can I restructure so it doesn't add complexity?\n\nOption: fold the gate check into the existing structure without adding a branch at the top level. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: instead of early-continue, I could integrate the gate into the `match` via a helper that the loop calls — but any branch in the loop body counts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: what's the actual cognitive-complexity accounting? Cognitive complexity (Sonar): \n- `for` loop: +1\n- `if`: +1\n- `match`: +1 per match\n- `if-else`: +1 for if, +1 for else if... \n- nesting increments (if inside loop: +1 for the if +1 for nesting level = 2).\n\nThe function at baseline is 13. Adding an `if` at loop level (nesting depth 1 inside for) would add +1 (if) + 1 (nesting) = 2 → 15? But measured 14. Hmm, my gate `if` has a `continue` body (no else), so it's just +1 for the if +1 nesting = 2 → 15. But it measured 14. Let me not over-analyze; the measured values: baseline 13, mine 14. So it went up by 1.\n\nCan I restructure to avoid increasing? \n\nIdea: extract the whole per-fragment handling into a helper function `check_fragment(f) -> Vec<String>`, so the loop just becomes:\n```\nfor f in &fragments {\n    failures.extend(check_fragment(f));\n}\n```\nBut that changes the loop body and would move complexity to the helper — the helper would then have its own complexity budget. But the helper `check_fragment` would contain the match with all arms, similar complexity to the original function (minus the loop). That would make `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` much simpler (loop + slow handling) but the new helper would be ~12-13 complexity. That doesn't help — it just moves the standing warning.\n\nHmm. Actually, the cleanest approach that doesn't increase complexity: incorporate the gate into the existing `if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled()` line? No.\n\nAlternative: Put the gate check inside `compile_only_failures`-style dispatch differently. \n\nWhat if I restructure so the gate is handled by the same match but through Outcome transformation? For a gated fragment, we want: compile must fail with casing message. This is essentially a different \"outcome\" for the fragment. \n\nCleanest: preprocess — build a map of gated `at` → and in the loop, choose the check function via a small dispatch. But that's still a branch.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the requirement. The brief says \"`just clippy` clean on code you touched\". Given the repo has 32+ standing clippy warnings and this specific function already warned at 13/10 before I touched it, the practical interpretation is: don't make it meaningfully worse. Going 13→14 is +1.\n\nBut actually, maybe I can restructure the gate to not add a top-level branch. What if the gate check replaces part of the existing slow-check line? Like:\n\n```\nif f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n    failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nI could add the gate to the loop but count-minimize. The cognitive complexity for `if` inside a `for` at nesting level 1 is 1 (if) + 1 (nesting) = 2. But measured only +1. Whatever.\n\nLet me think differently: extract the gated check into the outcome `match` itself. E.g.:\n\n```\nmatch &f.outcome {\n    _ if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) => failures.extend(gated_failures(f)),\n    Outcome::Output(want) => ...,\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nA match arm guard... does a guard count? Cognitive complexity: a `match` with a guard — the guard might add complexity. Actually let me test empirically.\n\nSimplest robust approach: extract the per-fragment logic into its own function and accept the standing warning moves there — no, that's shuffling.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the +1 matters at all. Let me re-read the brief: \"`just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on code you touched\". Given the baseline already emits this exact warning on this exact function (13/10), and the repo's own clippy.toml documents standing findings, the spirit is \"don't add new classes of problems\". +1 on an already-warning function is marginal.\n\nBut I can do better and actually reduce: what if the gate logic replaces the early-`continue` with a match-guard so it doesn't add a separate if? Let me test the cognitive complexity of a match-arm guard approach.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The function already has a `match &f.outcome` with a guard arm `Err(e) if ...` inside. Adding `_ if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) => ...` as the first arm: a wildcard arm with a guard. The `_` pattern with a guard — cognitive complexity counts match +1, and each guarded arm might add +1 for the guard? Let me just try it and measure.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider a cleaner design that reduces complexity overall: extract the outcome handling into a separate function `check_outcome(f) -> Vec<String>`, which includes the gate as its first arm. Then the loop:\n\n```\nfor f in &fragments {\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n        failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n        continue;\n    }\n    failures.extend(check_outcome(f));\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the gate should be checked before the slow check (a gated fragment bypasses slow). Actually none of the gated fragments are slow, so order doesn't matter functionally. But logically gate-first is cleaner. If I put gate inside check_outcome, then a slow+gated fragment would go through compile_only_failures first — but there are none, so fine. Actually, to keep semantics clean (gate short-circuits everything), I'd want gate checked before slow. But since no gated fragment is slow, and the gate is temporary, I think it's acceptable to check the gate inside check_outcome and keep the slow check in the loop.\n\nHmm, but then `check_outcome` would contain the big match with the gate arm guard — complexity of check_outcome ≈ 12. And every_fragment becomes: for + if + if-let? Let me count the new every_fragment:\n```\nfor f in &fragments {           // +1 for\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {   // +1 if +1 nesting? = 2\n        failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n        continue;\n    }\n    failures.extend(check_outcome(f));\n}\n```\nThat's ~3. And check_outcome gets the match (~12 with nesting). Total \"worst function\" would be check_outcome at ~12 — still over 10 but that's the standing situation. This just relocates the standing warning and adds a new function. Not obviously better.\n\nLet me just measure: does adding `_ if capital_variant_gate(&f.at)` as the first match arm change the complexity? Let me try it and see if clippy reports a different number.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what matters here. The repo has 32 standing clippy warnings; `just clippy` is NOT clean at baseline. So the brief's \"just clippy clean on code you touched\" cannot mean literally zero warnings. It most plausibly means \"don't introduce new clippy warnings\" / \"don't make things worse in a way you control\". My change nudged one already-warning function from 13 to 14. \n\nI think the right move is to restructure so the gate doesn't add complexity — try the match-arm-guard approach and measure. If that keeps it at 13, great. Let me try.\n\nActually, even better: I realize I can keep the early-continue structure but it's the natural Rust idiom. Let me just measure the guard approach.\n\nLet me modify the code: replace the early-continue block with a match arm guard.\n\nCurrent:\n```rust\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n            failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        match &f.outcome {\n            Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n```\n\nNew:\n```rust\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        match &f.outcome {\n            _ if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) => failures.extend(gated_failures(f)),\n            Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n```\n\nWait, but this changes the order: the gate is now after the slow check. If a fragment were both slow and gated, it'd go through compile_only_failures and report BROKEN. But there are no such fragments. Still, semantically the gate is a \"temporary override of the outcome\", so checking it in the match (instead of as a pre-check) is arguably cleaner: \"for gated fragments, the expectation is replaced by the gate check\". And it stays green.\n\nHmm, but actually putting it as `_ if ...` first arm is a bit odd since `_` matches everything. It's a common Rust pattern though. But cognitive complexity: the guard on `_` — let me measure.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. There might be an even simpler way: since the gate means \"the fragment's expectation is stale; check it still fails to compile for the casing reason\", I could just route gated fragments through a modified outcome in `extract`. E.g., when constructing the Fragment, if the `at` is gated, wrap its outcome in a special `Gated` variant. Then the match in the main loop handles `Outcome::Gated` like any other arm — no extra branch in the loop, no guard. The complexity of the match goes from 4 arms to 5 arms, and cognitive complexity doesn't count match arms! From clippy docs: \"match arms are not counted\" (the clippy.toml comment even