), it'd go through compile_only_failures. With Option B, Outcome::Gated and slow could coexist; the loop checks slow first, so a slow+gated fragment would be handled by compile_only_failures (reporting BROKEN does-not-compile) rather than gated_failures. Since none exist, moot. But to be safe/clean, I could keep the gate as a pre-loop check too... no, let's not over-engineer. No gated fragment is slow.\n\nHmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider: is it cleaner to keep the gate as an early-continue pre-check (Option C, current implementation) and just accept +1 complexity, or restructure to Option B (match arm, no complexity increase)?\n\nOption B is genuinely cleaner AND fixes the complexity regression. Let me go with Option B.\n\nLet me implement Option B:\n\n1. Add `Gated` variant to `Outcome`:\n```rust\nenum Outcome {\n    Output(String),\n    Refusal,\n    Error(String),\n    /// The gh:156 capital-variant gate: the page still spells lowercase variant names, so the\n    /// expectation fence is stale and the fragment is checked as gated instead (see\n    /// `capital_variant_gate` and `gated_failures`).\n    Gated,\n}\n```\n\n2. In `extract`, after computing `outcome`, build the Fragment, then if gated, replace outcome:\n```rust\nlet mut outcome = match info.trim() { ... };\nif capital_variant_gate(&at) {\n    outcome = Outcome::Gated;\n}\nfragments.push(Fragment { at: p.at, program: p.program, input: p.input, outcome, slow: p.slow });\n```\n\nWait — `at` here is the line of the expectation fence, not the toylang fence. I need `p.at` (the toylang opening fence line) for the gate check. So:\n```rust\nlet at = p.at.clone();\nlet mut outcome = match info.trim() { ... };\nif capital_variant_gate(&at) {\n    outcome = Outcome::Gated;\n}\nfragments.push(Fragment { at, program: p.program, input: p.input, outcome, slow: p.slow });\n```\nHmm wait, `at` was already defined at the top of extract as `format!(\"{page}:{}\", i + 1)` — that's the CURRENT fence (expectation). I need the toylang fence's at. The Pending struct holds `at: p.at`. So:\n\n```rust\n\"output\" | \"refuses\" | \"error\" => {\n    let Some(p) = pending.take() else { ... };\n    assert!(!p.slow || info.trim() == \"output\", ...);\n    let mut outcome = match info.trim() {\n        \"output\" => Outcome::Output(body),\n        \"error\" => Outcome::Error(body.trim_end().to_string()),\n        _ => { ...; Outcome::Refusal }\n    };\n    if capital_variant_gate(&p.at) {\n        outcome = Outcome::Gated;\n    }\n    fragments.push(Fragment { at: p.at, program: p.program, input: p.input, outcome, slow: p.slow });\n}\n```\n\n3. In the main loop, replace the gate pre-check with a plain match arm:\n```rust\nmatch &f.outcome {\n    Outcome::Gated => failures.extend(gated_failures(f)),\n    Outcome::Output(want) => ...,\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWait, but there's an ordering concern: the match arm for Gated. Since the match on `&f.outcome` is exhaustive and Gated is a distinct variant, fine.\n\nBut hold on — the slow check in the loop: `if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() { compile_only_failures(f); continue; }` comes before the match. If a gated fragment were slow (none), it'd skip the gate. Not a concern now.\n\n4. Remove the gate pre-check from the loop.\n\nThis adds zero cognitive complexity (new match arm, and `if capital_variant_gate` in extract — let me count extract's complexity). extract is a big function too; adding `if capital_variant_gate(&p.at) { outcome = Outcome::Gated; }` adds +1 (+1 if + nesting?) to extract's complexity. Let me check extract's current complexity at baseline.\n\nHmm, extract might also already be over threshold. Let me check. Actually, let me check the baseline clippy for extract (fn extract at ~line 242 originally, now shifted).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Both approaches add one `if` somewhere. Option B moves the `if` from the loop (already-warning function, 13→14) to extract. If extract is already warning or becomes warning, that's a wash or worse.\n\nLet me check extract's baseline complexity.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f079f800013fJy6Wrc2zl38g","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f066351001g3vtSJ8w1ENo9h"}}
