{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300816893,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b79fd0878a1a23b7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0 2>&1 | rg -n -A2 \"docs.rs\"","timeout":300000},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0 2>&1 | rg -n -A2 \"docs.rs\"","time":{"start":1788300816798,"end":1788300816891}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So with my current change, only `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` is flagged (14/10), and `extract` is not flagged (and I haven't touched it). The baseline had this function at 13/10 already flagged.\n\nNow, is +1 an acceptable outcome? The brief says \"just clippy clean on code you touched\". Since the baseline already flagged this function, \"clean\" can't mean zero. But I'd rather not make it worse. \n\nOption B (Gated variant) would put the `if` in extract, potentially creating a NEW warning there. Let me measure extract's current complexity to see if +4 would tip it over.\n\nLet me use clippy's `--message-format` to get exact numbers, or better: I can compute via clippy by temporarily... Actually, let me just measure: use clippy with `-W clippy::cognitive-complexity` and see if there's a way to get extract's number. Clippy only reports functions over the threshold. So extract is ≤10 currently.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. There's another Option D: keep the pre-check in the loop but make it not count as +1 by restructuring how the gate integrates.\n\nActually, thinking about it more: the +1 comes from adding one `if` in the loop body. What if I use the match-guard approach (Option A) — put `_ if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) => failures.extend(gated_failures(f))` as the first match arm, and remove the pre-check if? Then:\n- every_fragment: match with a guarded arm. Does the guard count? Let me check clippy docs: cognitive complexity counts \"guards\" — from the clippy.toml comment: \"clippy counts if/else/loops/guards but not match arms\". So a match arm guard DOES count! `_ if capital_variant_gate(...)` — the guard counts as +1 (and nesting? guards are at the match level). Hmm. So Option A might also add +1 or +2.\n\nLet me just empirically test Option A's effect on the number. Actually, let me think about what counts exactly. Cognitive complexity from SonarSource:\n- sequence: +0\n- if: +1 (and +1 more for each nesting level)\n- else if: +1 + nesting\n- ternary: +1\n- switch: +1, and each case: +0, but if there are 3+ cases, ... \n- loops: +1\n- catch: +1\n- goto/labels: +1\n- sequence of binary operators: each binary op after the first adds +1 (e.g., `a && b && c` = +2)\n- recursion: +1\n\nFor match with guards: I believe a guard counts like an `if`. \n\nThe nesting increment: cognitive complexity adds +1 for each nesting level when an element is inside another. E.g., an `if` inside a `for` inside a `while` = +1 (if) + 2 (nesting) = 3? Actually the formula: each control structure adds 1 + nesting_level (where nesting_level is the number of structures enclosing it).\n\nIn every_fragment, the loop is:\n```\nfor f in &fragments {                    // +1, nesting 0\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {  // +1 + 1(nesting inside for) = 2, plus the && doesn't add (single op)\n        ...\n        continue;\n    }\n    match &f.outcome {                   // +1 + 1 = 2\n        ...arms not counted...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — the baseline is 13. Let me not go down this rabbit hole; empirical measurement is faster.\n\nThe cleanest way to avoid ANY change to the existing function's complexity: put the gate check in a place that's already over-threshold or where the +1 is invisible... \n\nActually — here's the thing. The `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` function is the natural place for the gate, and it already warns at 13/10 (standing). Going 13→14 is a marginal increase on an already-warning function that the repo has chosen to tolerate. The alternative designs either add complexity to extract (currently clean, worse) or use a guard (also adds complexity).\n\nWait, let me reconsider Option B more carefully with the exact nesting. In extract, the code path is:\n```\nwhile let Some((i, line)) = lines.next() {       // while: +1\n    ...\n    match info.trim() {                           // match: +1 (nesting 1 → +2)\n        ...\n        \"output\" | \"refuses\" | \"error\" => {       // arm, not counted\n            ...\n            let mut outcome = match info.trim() { // inner match: +1 (nesting 2 → +3)\n                ...\n            };\n            if capital_variant_gate(&p.at) {      // if: +1 (nesting 3 → +4)\n                outcome = Outcome::Gated;\n            }\n            ...\n        }\n        ...\n    }\n}\n```\nThe `if` at nesting 3 adds +4. Extract baseline is unknown but ≤10 (not flagged). If it's ≥7, it becomes a new warning.\n\nSo Option B risks creating a new warning in extract. Not great either.\n\nLet me reconsider. What about making the gate check part of `gated_failures` dispatch in a way that doesn't add a control structure to any function? \n\nAlternative Option E: Handle gating inside the existing match arm structure using the already-existing guard pattern. Look at the Error arm:\n```rust\nOutcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n    Ok(_) => ...,\n    Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => ...,\n    Err(_) => {},\n},\n```\nThis already has a match with a guard inside. If I add the gate as `Err(e) if ...` style... no, the gate applies to all outcomes.\n\nHmm, let me step back and reconsider whether +1 really matters, or whether I should just accept the current clean, readable implementation. \n\nThe brief says \"just fmt and just clippy clean on code you touched\". Let me re-read the exact AGENTS.md guidance about clippy... Actually the brief (not AGENTS.md) says it. Given:\n1. The repo baseline has 32+ clippy warnings including this exact function at 13/10.\n2. The clippy.toml explicitly says thresholds are set where standing findings are \"countable rather than wallpaper\" — meaning these warnings are known and tolerated.\n3. My +1 is on an already-warning function.\n\nBut actually, I realize I should try harder to keep the count flat, because it's low-effort and the \"right\" thing. Let me try the match-arm-guard (Option A) and measure. If it's flat at 13, use it. If 14, revert to the current early-continue (also 14, but more readable).\n\nActually, wait — there's an even cleaner Option F: since `capital_variant_gate` is a pure predicate on `f.at`, and the gate replaces the outcome check, I can restructure so the loop picks the right check without an if: compute the effective outcome at Fragment-construction time in extract (Option B), BUT avoid the nested `if` by folding the gate decision into the `outcome` computation itself via the match:\n\nIn extract, the outcome computation:\n```rust\nlet outcome = match info.trim() {\n    \"output\" => Outcome::Output(body),\n    \"error\" => Outcome::Error(body.trim_end().to_string()),\n    _ => { assert!(body.is_empty(), ...); Outcome::Refusal }\n};\n```\nI could transform: if gated, Gated. But `capital_variant_gate` needs `p.at` and there's no natural place in this match. Adding it would need an if or a helper.\n\nHelper approach: \n```rust\nfn gated(at: &str, outcome: Outcome) -> Outcome {\n    if capital_variant_gate(at) { Outcome::Gated } else { outcome }\n}\n```\nThen in extract: `let outcome = gated(&p.at, match info.trim() { ... });`. This moves the `if` into a tiny helper `gated` — but `gated` itself has an `if` = complexity 1+? A top-level if = +1, way under 10. And extract gets NO new control structure (just a function call). This keeps extract's complexity unchanged! And the main loop gets a plain `Outcome::Gated` match arm (not counted).\n\nBut wait, does calling `gated(...)` with the outcome as an argument count in extract? No — function calls aren't counted. \n\nThis is clean:\n1. `capital_variant_gate(at)` — pure predicate, complexity 0.\n2. `gated(at, outcome) -> Outcome` — has an if, complexity 1 (trivial helper).\n3. `gated_failures(f)` — the gate check.\n4. In extract: `let outcome = gated(&p.at, <match>);` — extract unchanged complexity.\n5. In the loop: add `Outcome::Gated => failures.extend(gated_failures(f)),` — match arms not counted, so every_fragment stays at 13.\n\nHmm wait, but adding the `Gated` variant to Outcome and a match arm — the match on `&f.outcome` must be exhaustive; adding a new arm for Gated. Match arms aren't counted in cognitive complexity. So every_fragment stays 13/10 (baseline standing warning, unchanged by me).\n\nActually — hold on, is this over-engineering? Let me reconsider. The `gated` helper with a single `if` returning one of two values... that's a wrapper function. AGENTS.md code shape: \"Build for callers that exist.\" There's one caller. Hmm. A one-line `if` in the caller is simpler. But the caller (extract) has complexity implications.\n\nAlternatively, don't add a Gated variant to Outcome at all. Keep the loop pre-check (current approach) at 14/10. Accept +1.\n\nDecision time. Let me weigh:\n- Current approach (pre-check `if` in loop): +1 on an already-flagged function. Simple, clear, idiomatic.\n- Option B+F (Gated variant + helper): keeps every_fragment at 13, extract unchanged, adds a Gated enum variant + a tiny `gated` helper + gated_failures. More moving parts but zero net complexity change.\n\nThe cleanest minimal-surface solution that keeps clippy flat is B+F. But it adds an enum variant and helper. Is the added complexity of B+F justified? The gate is temporary (removed by doc-migrate-capital-variants). When removed, the Gated variant, helper, and arm all go away together. That's clean removal.\n\nActually, you know what — let me reconsider yet again. Let me look at what the cognitive-complexity would be for the current approach if I write it more carefully. The `if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) { ...; continue; }` at nesting level 1 (inside for) = +1 + 1 = 2, pushing 13→15? But it measured 14. So maybe the baseline 13 doesn't include nesting the way I think, or the `if` adds only +1 in this context. Whatever — empirically +1.\n\nLet me just go with Option B+F: it's principled (the gate is a property of the fragment, expressed as its outcome), keeps the numbers flat, and the module docstring already describes it. Let me implement.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider the placement of the helper. Actually — simplest expression: I don't even need a `gated` helper. In extract I can compute the outcome and immediately override:\n\n```rust\nlet mut outcome = match info.trim() { ... };\nif capital_variant_gate(&p.at) {\n    outcome = Outcome::Gated;\n}\n```\n\nThis is the direct `if` (complexity +4 to extract). To avoid that, the helper `gated` is needed. But the helper is fine — it's honest and tiny.\n\nHmm, actually — wait. Is extract's complexity really at risk? Let me measure extract's actual complexity number first before optimizing against a phantom. Let me lower the clippy threshold temporarily via env var to see extract's number. clippy.toml threshold is 10; I can pass `-D clippy::cognitive-complexity --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0`? Clippy config can be overridden on the command line with `-W clippy::cognitive-complexity` but the threshold comes from clippy.toml or `--config`. Let me try `cargo clippy -- -W clippy::cognitive-complexity --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0` to see ALL functions and their counts... actually `--config` on cargo clippy might not forward. Let me try.\n\nActually simpler: temporarily edit clippy.toml threshold to 0? No, don't touch files unnecessarily. Let me try the cargo clippy --config approach.\n\nActually, the cleanest measurement: use clippy with the lints allowed and read via `--message-format=json`? Overkill.\n\nLet me just try: `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0` and grep docs.rs. If clippy accepts `--config`, I'll see every function's number.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f08ebb7001n6RwxV7bUveS9h","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f07e4bb001O1DSYWY7Qt3AjG"}}
