{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788300818265,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05f08f751001AcFPaSDYUQaj1M","messageID":"msg_05f08f213001uGl7t4aKrW0XAc","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","snapshot":"adcb1963acc7fcb2c0adf120e8333c4b0a8e060c","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300825034,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-add2c3ced3105a61","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/checks/run.sh"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/checks/run.sh</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: #!/usr/bin/env bash\n2: #\n3: # Reports code-style findings for the files this session touched.\n4: #\n5: # Run from a Stop or SubagentStop hook: an agent that is about to finish is\n6: # told what it left behind, and pointed at the lesson for each finding. See\n7: # .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md.\n8: #\n9: # Adapted from kantord/toy-browser's .claude/checks/run.sh. The structural\n10: # change is what \"touched\" means: there it is whatever sits uncommitted at\n11: # stop time, which matches a flow where work waits for a human to commit.\n12: # Here AGENTS.md has sessions committing per step, so a session's tree is\n13: # clean at Stop and that definition would report nothing, every time. Touched\n14: # here is everything changed since `merge-base main HEAD`, plus anything\n15: # uncommitted, and \"before\" for the caused/inherited split is the merge-base\n16: # blob, not HEAD.\n17: #\n18: # Exit 0 = nothing to say. Exit 2 = findings, reported on stderr, which the\n19: # harness feeds back to the agent.\n20: \n21: set -uo pipefail\n22: \n23: root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0\n24: cd \"$root\" || exit 0\n25: \n26: # The harness sets this when it is re-firing after a block. Stopping twice for\n27: # the same thing would spin forever on a finding the agent cannot fix.\n28: if [ -t 0 ]; then\n29:   input=\"\"\n30: else\n31:   input=$(cat)\n32: fi\n33: if [ \"$(printf '%s' \"$input\" | jq -r '.stop_hook_active // false' 2>/dev/null)\" = \"true\" ]; then\n34:   exit 0\n35: fi\n36: \n37: limit=$(grep -oE '^max_file_lines[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*[0-9]+' .claude/checks/limits.toml 2>/dev/null | grep -oE '[0-9]+')\n38: : \"${limit:=1000}\"\n39: \n40: # Everything the repo owns and a person wrote: code, prose, scripts. Prose is\n41: # held to the same budget as code, which is what stops a document growing into\n42: # a wall nobody reads. Corpus YAML stays out: case shape is already enforced\n43: # harder than a hook could (unknown keys are errors, and tag_corpus.rs\n44: # rewrites node_types on every run).\n45: readonly OWNED=('*.rs' '*.md' '*.sh')\n46: \n47: # AGENTS.md has sessions commit their own work; ending a turn with a diff\n48: # still sitting in the tree is the politeness stall (issue #18), not a\n49: # decision anyone needs to make. Same reasoning as the rustfmt hook: one\n50: # right answer, so the finding is the fix itself, no lesson to look up.\n51: uncommitted=$(git status --porcelain -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\n52: if [ -n \"$uncommitted\" ]; then\n53:   {\n54:     echo \"Uncommitted work at Stop:\"\n55:     echo\n56:     echo \"$uncommitted\"\n57:     echo\n58:     echo \"Commit it before ending the turn -- per AGENTS.md, agents commit their own work here. This is not a code-style finding: there is no lesson to read, committing is the fix.\"\n59:   } >&2\n60:   exit 2\n61: fi\n62: \n63: # Files this session touched: committed on this branch since it left main,\n64: # plus anything uncommitted. Renames in status report as \"old -> new\", so the\n65: # last field is the path that exists now.\n66: base=$(git merge-base main HEAD 2>/dev/null) || base=\"\"\n67: mapfile -t touched < <(\n68:   {\n69:     [ -n \"$base\" ] && git diff --name-only \"$base\" -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null\n70:     git status --porcelain -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $NF}'\n71:   } | sort -u\n72: )\n73: \n74: # draft.md is excluded by name. Not because it is fine -- at ~2900 lines it is\n75: # exactly the wall the budget exists to prevent -- but because its shape is an\n76: # open decision (the board's draft-split-decision row), not something a\n77: # line-count hook should force on whichever session touches the draft next.\n78: kept=()\n79: for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n80:   [ \"$file\" = \"draft.md\" ] && continue\n81:   kept+=(\"$file\")\n82: done\n83: touched=(\"${kept[@]}\")\n84: [ \"${#touched[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && exit 0\n85: \n86: # finding lines are \"kind<TAB>path<TAB>detail\"\n87: findings=\"\"\n88: \n89: # Inline tests are measured apart from everything else. A file that inlines\n90: # its tests is not simpler than one that does not, so moving `#[cfg(test)]`\n91: # out to tests/ must not read as a split. Body and tests each get the full\n92: # budget, which caps the whole file at twice it without needing a third rule.\n93: # (Dormant today: no src/ file inlines tests. Harmless to keep.)\n94: inline_test_lines() {\n95:   awk '\n96:     /^[[:space:]]*#\\[cfg\\(test\\)\\]/ && !intest { intest = 1; depth = 0; opened = 0 }\n97:     intest {\n98:       count++\n99:       opens = gsub(/\\{/, \"{\"); closes = gsub(/\\}/, \"}\")\n100:       depth += opens - closes\n101:       if (opens > 0) opened = 1\n102:       if (opened && depth <= 0) intest = 0\n103:     }\n104:     END { print count + 0 }\n105:   ' \"$1\"\n106: }\n107: \n108: # Body/test line split for one file, per inline_test_lines above. Shared by the touched-file\n109: # loop and file_too_long_hit's whole-tree re-check below, so the budget math lives in one place.\n110: line_counts() {\n111:   local path=$1 lines tests\n112:   lines=$(wc -l < \"$path\" | tr -d ' ')\n113:   case \"$path\" in\n114:     *.rs) tests=$(inline_test_lines \"$path\") ;;\n115:     *) tests=0 ;;\n116:   esac\n117:   : \"${tests:=0}\"\n118:   printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$((lines - tests))\" \"$tests\"\n119: }\n120: \n121: for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n122:   [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n123:   IFS=$'\\t' read -r body tests < <(line_counts \"$file\")\n124: \n125:   # Was it already over before this branch? Debt you inherited is reported\n126:   # differently from debt you just created -- see the lesson.\n127:   before=$(git show \"${base:-HEAD}:$file\" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\n128:   if [ -z \"$before\" ] || [ \"$before\" = \"0\" ]; then\n129:     origin=\"new file\"\n130:   elif [ \"$before\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n131:     origin=\"inherited, already $before at merge-base\"\n132:   else\n133:     origin=\"caused, was $before at merge-base\"\n134:   fi\n135: \n136:   if [ \"$body\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n137:     findings+=\"file-too-long\t$file\t$body lines excluding inline tests, budget is $limit ($origin)\"$'\\n'\n138:   fi\n139:   if [ \"$tests\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n140:     findings+=\"file-too-long\t$file\t$tests lines of inline tests, budget is $limit ($origin)\"$'\\n'\n141:   fi\n142: done\n143: \n144: # Lessons and research-log notes are Open Knowledge Format bundles, and the\n145: # only thing that makes a document conformant is YAML frontmatter carrying a\n146: # `type`. Checking it here is what keeps \"valid OKF\" automatic rather than\n147: # remembered.\n148: #\n149: # Scoped to the lints bundle itself, not every skill: a case pattern's `*`\n150: # matches `/`, so `.claude/skills/*.md` used to also catch every\n151: # SKILL.md (#42) -- those carry Claude Code's own name/description\n152: # frontmatter, a different schema, and were never meant to have a `type`.\n153: for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n154:   case \"$file\" in\n155:     .claude/skills/code-style/lints/*.md | research-log/*.md) ;;\n156:     *) continue ;;\n157:   esac\n158:   [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n159:   if [ \"$(head -n 1 \"$file\")\" != \"---\" ]; then\n160:     findings+=\"okf-invalid\t$file\tno YAML frontmatter; OKF needs at least a type\"$'\\n'\n161:   elif ! awk 'NR>1 && /^---$/ {exit} NR>1' \"$file\" | grep -qE '^type:[[:space:]]*\\S'; then\n162:     findings+=\"okf-invalid\t$file\tfrontmatter has no type; OKF requires it\"$'\\n'\n163:   fi\n164: done\n165: \n166: # Clippy compiles the whole workspace, so its findings are gathered over all of it, not just\n167: # touched files, keeping `clippy_workspace` (kind, path, function, message) around for the\n168: # sinkhole's freeloader check below. Reporting still filters down to touched files. Warnings\n169: # only: a tree that does not compile is a different problem, reported elsewhere. The `function`\n170: # field is a best-effort read of the source line clippy points at (`fn NAME`, if that line has\n171: # one) -- empty when the span does not land on a signature line, which just means no\n172: # function-scoped sinkhole entry can match that finding.\n173: #\n174: # custom-build messages are dropped before anything else sees them: build.rs's #[path]-declared\n175: # modules (#73's prelude precompile) recompile ast.rs/error.rs/ty.rs/tir.rs/parse.rs/check/mod.rs\n176: # as a second, separate target, and a function build.rs's copy never calls is dead there even\n177: # though the lib target (compiled separately, in its own compiler-message block) uses it fine.\n178: # See issue #81.\n179: #\n180: # The trade the custom-build filter buys: any REAL finding in build.rs's own generator code\n181: # (not just the duplicated modules) is also invisible to this pass forever -- none exists\n182: # today, and the finer file-scoped filter was priced higher than that latent risk (gh:81).\n183: cargo_ran=0\n184: clippy_workspace=\"\"\n185: if command -v cargo > /dev/null 2>&1; then\n186:   cargo_ran=1\n187:   clippy_raw=$(cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --message-format=json -q 2>/dev/null \\\n188:     | jq -r 'select(.reason == \"compiler-message\")\n189: # The trade this buys: any REAL finding in code the build script itself generates (not just\n190: # the duplicated modules) is also invisible to this pass forever -- none exists today, and\n191: # the finer file-scoped filter was priced higher than that latent risk (gh:81).\n192:              | select((.target.kind // []) | index(\"custom-build\") | not)\n193:              | .message\n194:              | select(.level == \"warning\")\n195:              | select(.code.code != null)\n196:              | [(.code.code | sub(\"^clippy::\"; \"\") | gsub(\"_\"; \"-\")),\n197:                 (.spans[0].file_name // \"\"),\n198:                 (.spans[0].line_start // 0),\n199:                 .message]\n200:              | @tsv' 2>/dev/null)\n201: \n202:   while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path lineno detail; do\n203:     [ -n \"${path:-}\" ] || continue\n204:     func=\"\"\n205:     if [ -f \"$path\" ] && [ \"${lineno:-0}\" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then\n206:       func=$(sed -n \"${lineno}p\" \"$path\" | grep -oE '\\bfn[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*' | awk '{print $2}' | head -n1)\n207:     fi\n208:     detail_out=\"$detail\"\n209:     [ -n \"$func\" ] && detail_out=\"fn $func: $detail\"\n210:     clippy_workspace+=\"$kind\t$path\t$func\t$detail_out\"$'\\n'\n211:   done <<< \"$clippy_raw\"\n212:   clippy_workspace=$(printf '%s' \"$clippy_workspace\" | grep -v '^$')\n213: \n214:   while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path func detail; do\n215:     [ -n \"${path:-}\" ] || continue\n216:     for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n217:       if [ \"$file\" = \"$path\" ]; then\n218:         findings+=\"$kind\t$path\t$detail\"$'\\n'\n219:         break\n220:       fi\n221:     done\n222:   done <<< \"$clippy_workspace\"\n223: fi\n224: \n225: # `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it excuses is itself a finding -- the sinkhole\n226: # (.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml) is the only sanctioned home for a justified exemption. Anchored\n227: # at line start (after whitespace) so this does not fire on emit_rs.rs's two string literals,\n228: # which emit these tokens into *generated* Rust as `format!` arguments and never start a line\n229: # with them themselves. Also matches an allow riding in on cfg_attr (`#[cfg_attr(..., allow(...))]`),\n230: # which is a bare allow wearing a condition, not an exemption.\n231: bare_allow_pattern='^[[:space:]]*#!?\\[(allow\\(|cfg_attr\\(.*[,( ]allow\\()'\n232: for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n233:   case \"$file\" in\n234:     *.rs) ;;\n235:     *) continue ;;\n236:   esac\n237:   [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n238:   while IFS=: read -r lineno rest; do\n239:     [ -n \"${lineno:-}\" ] || continue\n240:     findings+=\"bare-allow\t$file\tline $lineno: $(printf '%s' \"$rest\" | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]+//')\"$'\\n'\n241:   done < <(grep -nE \"$bare_allow_pattern\" \"$file\" 2>/dev/null)\n242: done\n243: \n244: # `--all-targets` compiles lib and test targets separately, so the same\n245: # warning arrives once per target.\n246: findings=$(printf '%s' \"$findings\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\n247: \n248: # The sinkhole: structured, justified exemptions (.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml). Consulting it\n249: # means two things -- an entry suppresses the finding it excuses from blocking the agent, and\n250: # every entry is re-checked here, against the whole tree rather than just files this session\n251: # touched, so an exemption nobody needs any more (the function shrank, the file split) is itself\n252: # a finding instead of quietly outliving its reason.\n253: sinkhole_path=.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml\n254: if [ -f \"$sinkhole_path\" ] && command -v python3 > /dev/null 2>&1; then\n255:   entries=$(python3 - \"$sinkhole_path\" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null\n256: import sys, tomllib\n257: with open(sys.argv[1], \"rb\") as f:\n258:     data = tomllib.load(f)\n259: for e in data.get(\"exemption\", []):\n260:     print(\"\\t\".join([e.get(\"kind\", \"\"), e.get(\"path\", \"\"), e.get(\"function\", \"\")]))\n261: PY\n262:   )\n263: \n264:   # Whole-tree re-check, one function per entry kind. file-too-long re-derives via the same\n265:   # line_counts helper the touched-file loop uses. bare-allow re-runs the same pattern the\n266:   # finding loop above matched with, straight against the path -- no touched-file restriction.\n267:   # Everything else (too-many-lines and any bare clippy lint name) is matched against\n268:   # clippy_workspace by kind and path, scoped to a function when the entry names one; a\n269:   # too-many-lines entry is matched by function name, read off the source line clippy points at\n270:   # (the span lands exactly on the `fn` line for this lint, not a wrapped signature line).\n271:   file_too_long_hit() {\n272:     local path=$1 body tests\n273:     [ -f \"$path\" ] || return 1\n274:     IFS=$'\\t' read -r body tests < <(line_counts \"$path\")\n275:     [ \"$body\" -gt \"$limit\" ] || [ \"$tests\" -gt \"$limit\" ]\n276:   }\n277:   bare_allow_hit() {\n278:     local path=$1\n279:     [ -f \"$path\" ] || return 1\n280:     grep -qE \"$bare_allow_pattern\" \"$path\" 2>/dev/null\n281:   }\n282:   clippy_lint_hit() {\n283:     local kind=$1 path=$2 func=$3\n284:     [ \"$cargo_ran\" -eq 1 ] || return 0 # cargo unavailable: cannot re-check, do not evict on faith\n285:     printf '%s\\n' \"$clippy_workspace\" | awk -F'\\t' -v k=\"$kind\" -v p=\"$path\" -v fn=\"$func\" \\\n286:       '$1 == k && $2 == p && (fn == \"\" || $3 == fn) { found=1 } END { exit !found }'\n287:   }\n288: \n289:   remaining=\"\"\n290:   if [ -n \"$findings\" ]; then\n291:     while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path detail; do\n292:       matched=0\n293:       while IFS=$'\\t' read -r ekind epath efunc; do\n294:         [ -n \"${ekind:-}\" ] || continue\n295:         [ \"$kind\" = \"$ekind\" ] && [ \"$path\" = \"$epath\" ] || continue\n296:         if [ -n \"$efunc\" ]; then\n297:           case \"$detail\" in\n298:             \"fn $efunc: \"*) ;;\n299:             *) continue ;;\n300:           esac\n301:         fi\n302:         matched=1\n303:         break\n304:       done <<< \"$entries\"\n305:       [ \"$matched\" -eq 0 ] && remaining+=\"$kind\t$path\t$detail\"$'\\n'\n306:     done <<< \"$findings\"\n307:   fi\n308:   findings=$(printf '%s' \"$remaining\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\n309: \n310:   while IFS=$'\\t' read -r ekind epath efunc; do\n311:     [ -n \"${ekind:-}\" ] || continue\n312:     live=0\n313:     case \"$ekind\" in\n314:       file-too-long) file_too_long_hit \"$epath\" && live=1 ;;\n315:       bare-allow) bare_allow_hit \"$epath\" && live=1 ;;\n316:       *) clippy_lint_hit \"$ekind\" \"$epath\" \"$efunc\" && live=1 ;;\n317:     esac\n318:     if [ \"$live\" -eq 0 ]; then\n319:       target=\"$epath\"\n320:       [ -n \"$efunc\" ] && target=\"$epath ($efunc)\"\n321:       findings+=$'\\n'\"stale-sinkhole-entry\t$sinkhole_path\tentry for $ekind on $target no longer fires -- the exemption has nothing left to excuse, remove it\"\n322:     fi\n323:   done <<< \"$entries\"\n324:   findings=$(printf '%s' \"$findings\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\n325: fi\n326: \n327: [ -z \"$findings\" ] && exit 0\n328: \n329: count=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$findings\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\n330: {\n331:   echo \"Code style: $count finding(s) in files this session touched.\"\n332:   echo\n333:   while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path detail; do\n334:     lesson=\".claude/skills/code-style/lints/$kind.md\"\n335:     echo \"  $kind  $path\"\n336:     echo \"    $detail\"\n337:     if [ -f \"$lesson\" ]; then\n338:       echo \"    lesson: $lesson\"\n339:     else\n340:       echo \"    lesson: $lesson  (MISSING -- nobody has decided how to handle this)\"\n341:     fi\n342:     echo\n343:   done <<< \"$findings\"\n344:   echo \"Read .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md before acting on any of these.\"\n345: } >&2\n346: \n347: exit 2\n\n(End of file - total 347 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n#\n# Reports code-style findings for the files this session touched.\n#\n# Run from a Stop or SubagentStop hook: an agent that is about to finish is\n# told what it left behind, and pointed at the lesson for each finding. See\n# .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md.\n#\n# Adapted from kantord/toy-browser's .claude/checks/run.sh. The structural\n# change is what \"touched\" means: there it is whatever sits uncommitted at\n# stop time, which matches a flow where work waits for a human to commit.\n# Here AGENTS.md has sessions committing per step, so a session's tree is\n# clean at Stop and that definition would report nothing, every time. Touched\n# here is everything changed since `merge-base main HEAD`, plus anything\n# uncommitted, and \"before\" for the caused/inherited split is the merge-base\n# blob, not HEAD.\n#\n# Exit 0 = nothing to say. Exit 2 = findings, reported on stderr, which the\n# harness feeds back to the agent.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/checks/run.sh","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n#\n# Reports code-style findings for the files this session touched.\n#\n# Run from a Stop or SubagentStop hook: an agent that is about to finish is\n# told what it left behind, and pointed at the lesson for each finding. See\n# .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md.\n#\n# Adapted from kantord/toy-browser's .claude/checks/run.sh. The structural\n# change is what \"touched\" means: there it is whatever sits uncommitted at\n# stop time, which matches a flow where work waits for a human to commit.\n# Here AGENTS.md has sessions committing per step, so a session's tree is\n# clean at Stop and that definition would report nothing, every time. Touched\n# here is everything changed since `merge-base main HEAD`, plus anything\n# uncommitted, and \"before\" for the caused/inherited split is the merge-base\n# blob, not HEAD.\n#\n# Exit 0 = nothing to say. Exit 2 = findings, reported on stderr, which the\n# harness feeds back to the agent.\n\nset -uo pipefail\n\nroot=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0\ncd \"$root\" || exit 0\n\n# The harness sets this when it is re-firing after a block. Stopping twice for\n# the same thing would spin forever on a finding the agent cannot fix.\nif [ -t 0 ]; then\n  input=\"\"\nelse\n  input=$(cat)\nfi\nif [ \"$(printf '%s' \"$input\" | jq -r '.stop_hook_active // false' 2>/dev/null)\" = \"true\" ]; then\n  exit 0\nfi\n\nlimit=$(grep -oE '^max_file_lines[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*[0-9]+' .claude/checks/limits.toml 2>/dev/null | grep -oE '[0-9]+')\n: \"${limit:=1000}\"\n\n# Everything the repo owns and a person wrote: code, prose, scripts. Prose is\n# held to the same budget as code, which is what stops a document growing into\n# a wall nobody reads. Corpus YAML stays out: case shape is already enforced\n# harder than a hook could (unknown keys are errors, and tag_corpus.rs\n# rewrites node_types on every run).\nreadonly OWNED=('*.rs' '*.md' '*.sh')\n\n# AGENTS.md has sessions commit their own work; ending a turn with a diff\n# still sitting in the tree is the politeness stall (issue #18), not a\n# decision anyone needs to make. Same reasoning as the rustfmt hook: one\n# right answer, so the finding is the fix itself, no lesson to look up.\nuncommitted=$(git status --porcelain -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null)\nif [ -n \"$uncommitted\" ]; then\n  {\n    echo \"Uncommitted work at Stop:\"\n    echo\n    echo \"$uncommitted\"\n    echo\n    echo \"Commit it before ending the turn -- per AGENTS.md, agents commit their own work here. This is not a code-style finding: there is no lesson to read, committing is the fix.\"\n  } >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\n# Files this session touched: committed on this branch since it left main,\n# plus anything uncommitted. Renames in status report as \"old -> new\", so the\n# last field is the path that exists now.\nbase=$(git merge-base main HEAD 2>/dev/null) || base=\"\"\nmapfile -t touched < <(\n  {\n    [ -n \"$base\" ] && git diff --name-only \"$base\" -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null\n    git status --porcelain -- \"${OWNED[@]}\" 2>/dev/null | awk '{print $NF}'\n  } | sort -u\n)\n\n# draft.md is excluded by name. Not because it is fine -- at ~2900 lines it is\n# exactly the wall the budget exists to prevent -- but because its shape is an\n# open decision (the board's draft-split-decision row), not something a\n# line-count hook should force on whichever session touches the draft next.\nkept=()\nfor file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n  [ \"$file\" = \"draft.md\" ] && continue\n  kept+=(\"$file\")\ndone\ntouched=(\"${kept[@]}\")\n[ \"${#touched[@]}\" -eq 0 ] && exit 0\n\n# finding lines are \"kind<TAB>path<TAB>detail\"\nfindings=\"\"\n\n# Inline tests are measured apart from everything else. A file that inlines\n# its tests is not simpler than one that does not, so moving `#[cfg(test)]`\n# out to tests/ must not read as a split. Body and tests each get the full\n# budget, which caps the whole file at twice it without needing a third rule.\n# (Dormant today: no src/ file inlines tests. Harmless to keep.)\ninline_test_lines() {\n  awk '\n    /^[[:space:]]*#\\[cfg\\(test\\)\\]/ && !intest { intest = 1; depth = 0; opened = 0 }\n    intest {\n      count++\n      opens = gsub(/\\{/, \"{\"); closes = gsub(/\\}/, \"}\")\n      depth += opens - closes\n      if (opens > 0) opened = 1\n      if (opened && depth <= 0) intest = 0\n    }\n    END { print count + 0 }\n  ' \"$1\"\n}\n\n# Body/test line split for one file, per inline_test_lines above. Shared by the touched-file\n# loop and file_too_long_hit's whole-tree re-check below, so the budget math lives in one place.\nline_counts() {\n  local path=$1 lines tests\n  lines=$(wc -l < \"$path\" | tr -d ' ')\n  case \"$path\" in\n    *.rs) tests=$(inline_test_lines \"$path\") ;;\n    *) tests=0 ;;\n  esac\n  : \"${tests:=0}\"\n  printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$((lines - tests))\" \"$tests\"\n}\n\nfor file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n  [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n  IFS=$'\\t' read -r body tests < <(line_counts \"$file\")\n\n  # Was it already over before this branch? Debt you inherited is reported\n  # differently from debt you just created -- see the lesson.\n  before=$(git show \"${base:-HEAD}:$file\" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\n  if [ -z \"$before\" ] || [ \"$before\" = \"0\" ]; then\n    origin=\"new file\"\n  elif [ \"$before\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n    origin=\"inherited, already $before at merge-base\"\n  else\n    origin=\"caused, was $before at merge-base\"\n  fi\n\n  if [ \"$body\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n    findings+=\"file-too-long\t$file\t$body lines excluding inline tests, budget is $limit ($origin)\"$'\\n'\n  fi\n  if [ \"$tests\" -gt \"$limit\" ]; then\n    findings+=\"file-too-long\t$file\t$tests lines of inline tests, budget is $limit ($origin)\"$'\\n'\n  fi\ndone\n\n# Lessons and research-log notes are Open Knowledge Format bundles, and the\n# only thing that makes a document conformant is YAML frontmatter carrying a\n# `type`. Checking it here is what keeps \"valid OKF\" automatic rather than\n# remembered.\n#\n# Scoped to the lints bundle itself, not every skill: a case pattern's `*`\n# matches `/`, so `.claude/skills/*.md` used to also catch every\n# SKILL.md (#42) -- those carry Claude Code's own name/description\n# frontmatter, a different schema, and were never meant to have a `type`.\nfor file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n  case \"$file\" in\n    .claude/skills/code-style/lints/*.md | research-log/*.md) ;;\n    *) continue ;;\n  esac\n  [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n  if [ \"$(head -n 1 \"$file\")\" != \"---\" ]; then\n    findings+=\"okf-invalid\t$file\tno YAML frontmatter; OKF needs at least a type\"$'\\n'\n  elif ! awk 'NR>1 && /^---$/ {exit} NR>1' \"$file\" | grep -qE '^type:[[:space:]]*\\S'; then\n    findings+=\"okf-invalid\t$file\tfrontmatter has no type; OKF requires it\"$'\\n'\n  fi\ndone\n\n# Clippy compiles the whole workspace, so its findings are gathered over all of it, not just\n# touched files, keeping `clippy_workspace` (kind, path, function, message) around for the\n# sinkhole's freeloader check below. Reporting still filters down to touched files. Warnings\n# only: a tree that does not compile is a different problem, reported elsewhere. The `function`\n# field is a best-effort read of the source line clippy points at (`fn NAME`, if that line has\n# one) -- empty when the span does not land on a signature line, which just means no\n# function-scoped sinkhole entry can match that finding.\n#\n# custom-build messages are dropped before anything else sees them: build.rs's #[path]-declared\n# modules (#73's prelude precompile) recompile ast.rs/error.rs/ty.rs/tir.rs/parse.rs/check/mod.rs\n# as a second, separate target, and a function build.rs's copy never calls is dead there even\n# though the lib target (compiled separately, in its own compiler-message block) uses it fine.\n# See issue #81.\n#\n# The trade the custom-build filter buys: any REAL finding in build.rs's own generator code\n# (not just the duplicated modules) is also invisible to this pass forever -- none exists\n# today, and the finer file-scoped filter was priced higher than that latent risk (gh:81).\ncargo_ran=0\nclippy_workspace=\"\"\nif command -v cargo > /dev/null 2>&1; then\n  cargo_ran=1\n  clippy_raw=$(cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --message-format=json -q 2>/dev/null \\\n    | jq -r 'select(.reason == \"compiler-message\")\n# The trade this buys: any REAL finding in code the build script itself generates (not just\n# the duplicated modules) is also invisible to this pass forever -- none exists today, and\n# the finer file-scoped filter was priced higher than that latent risk (gh:81).\n             | select((.target.kind // []) | index(\"custom-build\") | not)\n             | .message\n             | select(.level == \"warning\")\n             | select(.code.code != null)\n             | [(.code.code | sub(\"^clippy::\"; \"\") | gsub(\"_\"; \"-\")),\n                (.spans[0].file_name // \"\"),\n                (.spans[0].line_start // 0),\n                .message]\n             | @tsv' 2>/dev/null)\n\n  while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path lineno detail; do\n    [ -n \"${path:-}\" ] || continue\n    func=\"\"\n    if [ -f \"$path\" ] && [ \"${lineno:-0}\" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then\n      func=$(sed -n \"${lineno}p\" \"$path\" | grep -oE '\\bfn[[:space:]]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*' | awk '{print $2}' | head -n1)\n    fi\n    detail_out=\"$detail\"\n    [ -n \"$func\" ] && detail_out=\"fn $func: $detail\"\n    clippy_workspace+=\"$kind\t$path\t$func\t$detail_out\"$'\\n'\n  done <<< \"$clippy_raw\"\n  clippy_workspace=$(printf '%s' \"$clippy_workspace\" | grep -v '^$')\n\n  while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path func detail; do\n    [ -n \"${path:-}\" ] || continue\n    for file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n      if [ \"$file\" = \"$path\" ]; then\n        findings+=\"$kind\t$path\t$detail\"$'\\n'\n        break\n      fi\n    done\n  done <<< \"$clippy_workspace\"\nfi\n\n# `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it excuses is itself a finding -- the sinkhole\n# (.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml) is the only sanctioned home for a justified exemption. Anchored\n# at line start (after whitespace) so this does not fire on emit_rs.rs's two string literals,\n# which emit these tokens into *generated* Rust as `format!` arguments and never start a line\n# with them themselves. Also matches an allow riding in on cfg_attr (`#[cfg_attr(..., allow(...))]`),\n# which is a bare allow wearing a condition, not an exemption.\nbare_allow_pattern='^[[:space:]]*#!?\\[(allow\\(|cfg_attr\\(.*[,( ]allow\\()'\nfor file in \"${touched[@]}\"; do\n  case \"$file\" in\n    *.rs) ;;\n    *) continue ;;\n  esac\n  [ -f \"$file\" ] || continue\n  while IFS=: read -r lineno rest; do\n    [ -n \"${lineno:-}\" ] || continue\n    findings+=\"bare-allow\t$file\tline $lineno: $(printf '%s' \"$rest\" | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]+//')\"$'\\n'\n  done < <(grep -nE \"$bare_allow_pattern\" \"$file\" 2>/dev/null)\ndone\n\n# `--all-targets` compiles lib and test targets separately, so the same\n# warning arrives once per target.\nfindings=$(printf '%s' \"$findings\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\n\n# The sinkhole: structured, justified exemptions (.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml). Consulting it\n# means two things -- an entry suppresses the finding it excuses from blocking the agent, and\n# every entry is re-checked here, against the whole tree rather than just files this session\n# touched, so an exemption nobody needs any more (the function shrank, the file split) is itself\n# a finding instead of quietly outliving its reason.\nsinkhole_path=.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml\nif [ -f \"$sinkhole_path\" ] && command -v python3 > /dev/null 2>&1; then\n  entries=$(python3 - \"$sinkhole_path\" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null\nimport sys, tomllib\nwith open(sys.argv[1], \"rb\") as f:\n    data = tomllib.load(f)\nfor e in data.get(\"exemption\", []):\n    print(\"\\t\".join([e.get(\"kind\", \"\"), e.get(\"path\", \"\"), e.get(\"function\", \"\")]))\nPY\n  )\n\n  # Whole-tree re-check, one function per entry kind. file-too-long re-derives via the same\n  # line_counts helper the touched-file loop uses. bare-allow re-runs the same pattern the\n  # finding loop above matched with, straight against the path -- no touched-file restriction.\n  # Everything else (too-many-lines and any bare clippy lint name) is matched against\n  # clippy_workspace by kind and path, scoped to a function when the entry names one; a\n  # too-many-lines entry is matched by function name, read off the source line clippy points at\n  # (the span lands exactly on the `fn` line for this lint, not a wrapped signature line).\n  file_too_long_hit() {\n    local path=$1 body tests\n    [ -f \"$path\" ] || return 1\n    IFS=$'\\t' read -r body tests < <(line_counts \"$path\")\n    [ \"$body\" -gt \"$limit\" ] || [ \"$tests\" -gt \"$limit\" ]\n  }\n  bare_allow_hit() {\n    local path=$1\n    [ -f \"$path\" ] || return 1\n    grep -qE \"$bare_allow_pattern\" \"$path\" 2>/dev/null\n  }\n  clippy_lint_hit() {\n    local kind=$1 path=$2 func=$3\n    [ \"$cargo_ran\" -eq 1 ] || return 0 # cargo unavailable: cannot re-check, do not evict on faith\n    printf '%s\\n' \"$clippy_workspace\" | awk -F'\\t' -v k=\"$kind\" -v p=\"$path\" -v fn=\"$func\" \\\n      '$1 == k && $2 == p && (fn == \"\" || $3 == fn) { found=1 } END { exit !found }'\n  }\n\n  remaining=\"\"\n  if [ -n \"$findings\" ]; then\n    while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path detail; do\n      matched=0\n      while IFS=$'\\t' read -r ekind epath efunc; do\n        [ -n \"${ekind:-}\" ] || continue\n        [ \"$kind\" = \"$ekind\" ] && [ \"$path\" = \"$epath\" ] || continue\n        if [ -n \"$efunc\" ]; then\n          case \"$detail\" in\n            \"fn $efunc: \"*) ;;\n            *) continue ;;\n          esac\n        fi\n        matched=1\n        break\n      done <<< \"$entries\"\n      [ \"$matched\" -eq 0 ] && remaining+=\"$kind\t$path\t$detail\"$'\\n'\n    done <<< \"$findings\"\n  fi\n  findings=$(printf '%s' \"$remaining\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\n\n  while IFS=$'\\t' read -r ekind epath efunc; do\n    [ -n \"${ekind:-}\" ] || continue\n    live=0\n    case \"$ekind\" in\n      file-too-long) file_too_long_hit \"$epath\" && live=1 ;;\n      bare-allow) bare_allow_hit \"$epath\" && live=1 ;;\n      *) clippy_lint_hit \"$ekind\" \"$epath\" \"$efunc\" && live=1 ;;\n    esac\n    if [ \"$live\" -eq 0 ]; then\n      target=\"$epath\"\n      [ -n \"$efunc\" ] && target=\"$epath ($efunc)\"\n      findings+=$'\\n'\"stale-sinkhole-entry\t$sinkhole_path\tentry for $ekind on $target no longer fires -- the exemption has nothing left to excuse, remove it\"\n    fi\n  done <<< \"$entries\"\n  findings=$(printf '%s' \"$findings\" | grep -v '^$' | sort -u)\nfi\n\n[ -z \"$findings\" ] && exit 0\n\ncount=$(printf '%s\\n' \"$findings\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\n{\n  echo \"Code style: $count finding(s) in files this session touched.\"\n  echo\n  while IFS=$'\\t' read -r kind path detail; do\n    lesson=\".claude/skills/code-style/lints/$kind.md\"\n    echo \"  $kind  $path\"\n    echo \"    $detail\"\n    if [ -f \"$lesson\" ]; then\n      echo \"    lesson: $lesson\"\n    else\n      echo \"    lesson: $lesson  (MISSING -- nobody has decided how to handle this)\"\n    fi\n    echo\n  done <<< \"$findings\"\n  echo \"Read .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md before acting on any of these.\"\n} >&2\n\nexit 2","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":347,"totalLines":347,"truncated":false}},"title":".claude/checks/run.sh","time":{"start":1788300825026,"end":1788300825032}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The `--config` didn't forward to clippy (it's a rustc flag, not accepted there, or it silently ignored). Let me try a different way: temporarily modify clippy.toml? I shouldn't leave changes, but I can test then revert. Actually, a cleaner way: clippy has `-A` / `-W` but threshold comes from clippy.toml only. Let me use a config override via env: clippy reads `CLIPPY_CONF_DIR`? No.\n\nLet me just create a temporary clippy.toml override... but I can't write files except with my write tool, and I shouldn't create scratch files in the repo. Actually I CAN write to /tmp? No — \"writes under /tmp\" is denied. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: I'll just implement Option B+F and measure both functions with the normal run. Since the approach is sound (match arms not counted, helper has an if at top level = +1 only), extract stays as-is and every_fragment stays 13.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether this whole complexity worry is worth it. Let me look at how the repo's Stop hook / checks treat clippy. The justfile `checks` runs `bash .claude/checks/run.sh`. Let me check what that does — if clippy isn't part of the gate, then the complexity concern is purely about \"code you touched\" cleanliness.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f090f990017xwTHAGxivtO0j","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f08f213001uGl7t4aKrW0XAc"}}
