{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788300826762,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05f0918840019fRQU9omMAs80s","messageID":"msg_05f0911de001J0nuwDYQcGRV5q","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","snapshot":"adcb1963acc7fcb2c0adf120e8333c4b0a8e060c","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300830766,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8445018e585e2c40","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat .claude/checks/sinkhole.toml 2>/dev/null; ls .claude/checks/"},"output":"# Structured exemptions for the check machinery.\n#\n# A finding a session cannot fix and should not chase -- a budget the codebase has decided, in\n# the open, to exceed on purpose -- moves here instead of staying an #[allow] beside the code or\n# a standing \"read the lesson and move on\" note in a lints/*.md file. Piece 7 of\n# plans/quality-practices.md is the design; this is its first tenant.\n#\n# What makes an exemption legitimate:\n#\n# - It is a decision, not a session's improvisation. See .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md's\n#   \"Suppressing a finding\" -- escalate, get it settled, then it lands here.\n# - `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it excuses is itself a finding (kind `bare-allow`,\n#   enforced in .claude/checks/run.sh). This file is the only sanctioned home for a justified\n#   exemption.\n# - `.claude/checks/run.sh` re-derives an entry's underlying finding on each run, over the\n#   whole tree, not just the files a session touched -- an entry whose finding no longer fires\n#   is itself reported, as `stale-sinkhole-entry`. Every kind is re-checked: `file-too-long` and\n#   `bare-allow` re-run their own detection directly, and everything else (`too-many-lines` and\n#   any bare clippy lint name) is matched against the clippy run gathered for this pass.\n#\n# Fields:\n#\n#   kind            required. The finding kind this excuses: \"file-too-long\",\n#                   \"too-many-lines\", \"bare-allow\", or a bare clippy lint name\n#                   (e.g. \"redundant-clone\").\n#   path            required. Repo-relative path.\n#   function        optional. Scopes a per-function kind (today: \"too-many-lines\") to one\n#                   function by name. Omit for a whole-file kind like \"file-too-long\".\n#   line            optional, informational only. Where the exemption lived when this was\n#                   written; drifts as the file changes, never used for matching.\n#   justification   required. Why this stays over budget by design, not by neglect.\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"file-too-long\"\npath = \"src/check/mod.rs\"\njustification = \"\"\"\nOver the 1000-line budget (.claude/checks/limits.toml) by design -- around 2200 lines as of\nthe #62 landing, and the exact count is not load-bearing: it has drifted stale twice, and\nwhat the exemption covers is the fused core's existence, not a number. check.rs is one of the\nthree files that budget was set to name (clippy.toml's too-many-lines comment), and the survey\nrun on it (plans/checker-structure.md, gh:32) settled the file's shape: extract the\ntype-resolution pass and the stream-linearity/pruning passes (board row check-rs-split-build,\ngh:51, not yet landed), and leave the fused synth/expect core -- Ctx, synth, expect, access,\nbinary, construct, collect, the mapper helpers -- as a structured exemption rather than a shrink\ntarget. The core has no per-area seam to split along (one flat match over every construct, kept\ncoherent on purpose), and the type-flow rework (#44) specifically wants each construct's synth\nside and expect side adjacent, which a topic-based split would pull apart. #51 landed: the two\npasses left for check/types.rs and check/linearity.rs, and what remains here is the fused core\nthis entry was always going to cover.\n\"\"\"\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"bare-allow\"\npath = \"tests/support/mod.rs\"\nline = 11\njustification = \"\"\"\nThe #![allow(dead_code)] plans/quality-practices.md gated this machinery on resolving: every\nintegration-test binary compiles support/mod.rs and uses a different subset of its helpers, so\nper-binary dead_code warnings are structural to Rust's test layout, not debt. Resolving the\ngate by sinkholing it is the machinery applied to its own precondition -- the allow moves from\nan unexplained attribute to a justified entry. The re-check keeps this honest like any\nother kind now: if the allow is ever deleted, this entry reports itself stale.\n\"\"\"\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"too-many-lines\"\npath = \"src/check/mod.rs\"\nfunction = \"synth\"\nline = 925\njustification = \"\"\"\nInherited, never caused, by the same history as check() above --\n.claude/skills/code-style/lints/too-many-lines.md records synth() at every measurement from 560\nlines (its first appearance in the lesson) down to today's count -- net downward, with the\nrecorded upticks belonging to sessions the lesson classifies as inheriting, not causing. It is the one large match over every Expr variant\n(plans/checker-structure.md's bidirectional-checker axis): a type checker enumerates the\nlanguage's grammar exactly once, with no per-area repetition to hang a split on the way the\nbackends get one file per target for free. research-log/merging-passes-turns-redundant-traversals-into-bugs.md\nis why checking and lowering stay fused inside it rather than becoming two smaller passes.\n\"\"\"\nformat.sh\nlimits.toml\nrun.sh\nsinkhole.toml\n","metadata":{"output":"# Structured exemptions for the check machinery.\n#\n# A finding a session cannot fix and should not chase -- a budget the codebase has decided, in\n# the open, to exceed on purpose -- moves here instead of staying an #[allow] beside the code or\n# a standing \"read the lesson and move on\" note in a lints/*.md file. Piece 7 of\n# plans/quality-practices.md is the design; this is its first tenant.\n#\n# What makes an exemption legitimate:\n#\n# - It is a decision, not a session's improvisation. See .claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md's\n#   \"Suppressing a finding\" -- escalate, get it settled, then it lands here.\n# - `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it excuses is itself a finding (kind `bare-allow`,\n#   enforced in .claude/checks/run.sh). This file is the only sanctioned home for a justified\n#   exemption.\n# - `.claude/checks/run.sh` re-derives an entry's underlying finding on each run, over the\n#   whole tree, not just the files a session touched -- an entry whose finding no longer fires\n#   is itself reported, as `stale-sinkhole-entry`. Every kind is re-checked: `file-too-long` and\n#   `bare-allow` re-run their own detection directly, and everything else (`too-many-lines` and\n#   any bare clippy lint name) is matched against the clippy run gathered for this pass.\n#\n# Fields:\n#\n#   kind            required. The finding kind this excuses: \"file-too-long\",\n#                   \"too-many-lines\", \"bare-allow\", or a bare clippy lint name\n#                   (e.g. \"redundant-clone\").\n#   path            required. Repo-relative path.\n#   function        optional. Scopes a per-function kind (today: \"too-many-lines\") to one\n#                   function by name. Omit for a whole-file kind like \"file-too-long\".\n#   line            optional, informational only. Where the exemption lived when this was\n#                   written; drifts as the file changes, never used for matching.\n#   justification   required. Why this stays over budget by design, not by neglect.\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"file-too-long\"\npath = \"src/check/mod.rs\"\njustification = \"\"\"\nOver the 1000-line budget (.claude/checks/limits.toml) by design -- around 2200 lines as of\nthe #62 landing, and the exact count is not load-bearing: it has drifted stale twice, and\nwhat the exemption covers is the fused core's existence, not a number. check.rs is one of the\nthree files that budget was set to name (clippy.toml's too-many-lines comment), and the survey\nrun on it (plans/checker-structure.md, gh:32) settled the file's shape: extract the\ntype-resolution pass and the stream-linearity/pruning passes (board row check-rs-split-build,\ngh:51, not yet landed), and leave the fused synth/expect core -- Ctx, synth, expect, access,\nbinary, construct, collect, the mapper helpers -- as a structured exemption rather than a shrink\ntarget. The core has no per-area seam to split along (one flat match over every construct, kept\ncoherent on purpose), and the type-flow rework (#44) specifically wants each construct's synth\nside and expect side adjacent, which a topic-based split would pull apart. #51 landed: the two\npasses left for check/types.rs and check/linearity.rs, and what remains here is the fused core\nthis entry was always going to cover.\n\"\"\"\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"bare-allow\"\npath = \"tests/support/mod.rs\"\nline = 11\njustification = \"\"\"\nThe #![allow(dead_code)] plans/quality-practices.md gated this machinery on resolving: every\nintegration-test binary compiles support/mod.rs and uses a different subset of its helpers, so\nper-binary dead_code warnings are structural to Rust's test layout, not debt. Resolving the\ngate by sinkholing it is the machinery applied to its own precondition -- the allow moves from\nan unexplained attribute to a justified entry. The re-check keeps this honest like any\nother kind now: if the allow is ever deleted, this entry reports itself stale.\n\"\"\"\n\n[[exemption]]\nkind = \"too-many-lines\"\npath = \"src/check/mod.rs\"\nfunction = \"synth\"\nline = 925\njustification = \"\"\"\nInherited, never caused, by the same history as check() above --\n.claude/skills/code-style/lints/too-many-lines.md records synth() at every measurement from 560\nlines (its first appearance in the lesson) down to today's count -- net downward, with the\nrecorded upticks belonging to sessions the lesson classifies as inheriting, not causing. It is the one large match over every Expr variant\n(plans/checker-structure.md's bidirectional-checker axis): a type checker enumerates the\nlanguage's grammar exactly once, with no per-area repetition to hang a split on the way the\nbackends get one file per target for free. research-log/merging-passes-turns-redundant-traversals-into-bugs.md\nis why checking and lowering stay fused inside it rather than becoming two smaller passes.\n\"\"\"\nformat.sh\nlimits.toml\nrun.sh\nsinkhole.toml\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat .claude/checks/sinkhole.toml 2>/dev/null; ls .claude/checks/","time":{"start":1788300830761,"end":1788300830765}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The Stop hook's checks only report clippy warnings on touched files (`tests/docs.rs`), and the sinkhole is the sanctioned home for justified exemptions. But the cognitive-complexity warning on `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` already exists at baseline (13/10), so it's a standing warning, not something I introduced fresh. Still, the finding would be reported on tests/docs.rs (a touched file). Let me check the sinkhole and the code-style skill to see how standing findings on touched files are handled.\n\nActually, let me read the code-style skill and sinkhole to understand whether a pre-existing warning on a touched file counts as \"caused\" or is tolerated.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f0925d5001xt5bfy9O4VXZ0N","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f0911de001J0nuwDYQcGRV5q"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300831499,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-89873bea3488d88d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: type: Playbook\n3: name: code-style\n4: title: Reacting to a code-style finding\n5: description: How to react when the code-style checks report a finding. Invoked by the Stop hook's message, not proactively; read it when a finding names it.\n6: tags: [code-style, hooks, lessons]\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # Reacting to a code-style finding\n10: \n11: A check ran over the files this session touched and found something. Each\n12: finding names a **kind**, and each kind has a **lesson**: a short file of\n13: worked examples recording how this repo has decided to handle it.\n14: \n15: ```\n16: file-too-long  src/emit_llvm.rs\n17:   1666 lines excluding inline tests, budget is 1000 (inherited, already 1666 at merge-base)\n18:   lesson: .claude/skills/code-style/lints/file-too-long.md\n19: ```\n20: \n21: The lessons are an [Open Knowledge Format](/.claude/skills/code-style/lints/README.md)\n22: bundle: plain markdown that cross-links into a graph. Start at the index\n23: there. The bundle currently ships empty, on purpose; the index says why. While\n24: it is empty, every finding leads here, and from here to *Escalate*.\n25: \n26: ## What to do\n27: \n28: 1. **Read the lesson.** If the file is missing, skip to *Escalate*.\n29: 2. **Follow its links.** A lesson is a small node, not a manual. It will point\n30:    at more specific ones; the prose around a link says what it is for.\n31: 3. **Find the case that matches your situation.** Some cases carry a note\n32:    saying they also fit other situations, or that a combination does.\n33: 4. **If one matches, follow it** and fix the finding.\n34: 5. **If none matches -- or what you found is too thin, too vague, or does not\n35:    actually tell you what to do here -- escalate.** Do not improvise.\n36: \n37: ## Escalate\n38: \n39: Escalating is the normal outcome for anything new. It is not a failure, and it\n40: is not something to avoid by picking the nearest example and hoping.\n41: \n42: **In a delegated session, escalate by filing a GitHub issue for the\n43: coordinator -- never by waiting for a human.** The maintainer interacts only\n44: with the drive loop's coordinator, not with delegated sessions, so an\n45: `AskUserQuestion` in a delegated session parks it forever. Instead: file an\n46: issue (`gh issue create`) stating what fired, why the lessons do not settle\n47: it, and the real alternatives with what each costs -- \"split by\n48: responsibility, or by kind of item, or leave it and record the exemption?\" is\n49: an escalation; \"how should I fix this?\" is not. Then take the most\n50: conservative continuation (leave the finding standing and the code as it is),\n51: commit everything that IS settled, and end the turn. The coordinator triages\n52: the issue: answers it where decided design already does, or turns it into a\n53: decide row on `plans/board.yaml` for a grilling session.\n54: \n55: **Only a session the maintainer is personally driving may `AskUserQuestion`\n56: directly.** A subagent can do neither -- it has no channel at all; it reports\n57: the escalation in its result and returns, and its spawner files the issue.\n58: \n59: Do not fix the finding, and do not finish the escalated part as if the\n60: question were not there.\n61: \n62: ## Writing what was settled\n63: \n64: Write the outcome into the lessons bundle. Three rules, in order of\n65: preference:\n66: \n67: 1. **Nothing new.** If an existing case already covers the situation, add one\n68:    line to it saying so. Prefer this.\n69: 2. **A line, not a section.** If a combination of existing cases covers it,\n70:    say which, in one sentence, where they are.\n71: 3. **A new node.** Only when the situation is genuinely new. Make it a *small\n72:    file of its own* and link it from the parent lesson -- do not grow the\n73:    parent. Where a toy-browser lesson holds the same repo-independent insight,\n74:    cite it as a source, per AGENTS.md's citing rule.\n75: \n76: Lessons are held to the same length budget as the code. That is deliberate:\n77: it is what forces specific knowledge into linked nodes instead of one wall of\n78: prose. Being as specific as you like is exactly what the graph is for.\n79: \n80: ## The file format\n81: \n82: Every lesson is a valid OKF v0.2 document: YAML frontmatter with at least\n83: `type`, then markdown.\n84: \n85: ```markdown\n86: ---\n87: type: Playbook\n88: title: Splitting an emitter that grew several jobs\n89: description: What to do when one backend file accumulated unrelated responsibilities.\n90: tags: [file-too-long, structure]\n91: ---\n92: \n93: emit_llvm.rs held codegen, its runtime shims and the type mapping...\n94: ```\n95: \n96: - `type` is the only required key. Use `Playbook` for \"how to handle X\",\n97:   `Reference` for a note others link to.\n98: - Link with plain markdown, bundle-relative:\n99:   `[the split we did for check.rs](/.claude/skills/code-style/lints/file-too-long/by-responsibility.md)`.\n100:   The link is untyped; the sentence around it carries the relationship.\n101: - Unknown keys are preserved by any conformant consumer, so this file's `name`\n102:   and `description` -- which the harness needs -- cost nothing.\n103: \n104: ## Suppressing a finding\n105: \n106: Sometimes the right answer is that the check is wrong for this case. That is\n107: also a decision worth recording: escalate, settle it, and write the exception\n108: into the lesson so the next agent does not re-litigate it.\n109: \n110: Never silence a finding by editing `limits.toml`, adding an `#[allow]`, or\n111: splitting a file into meaningless pieces to get under a number. The budget\n112: comes down over time by deliberate commits; it does not move to accommodate\n113: one session.\n114: \n115: Suppression is settled the same way as anything else, and almost never comes\n116: back \"yes\". If you believe a check is measuring nothing here, look for the\n117: better pattern first and bring *that* back -- an exemption is what is left\n118: when the search failed, argued for in the open, never something a session\n119: decides on its own to get unblocked.\n120: \n121: A settled exemption moves into `.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml`: one entry per\n122: finding it excuses, with a `justification` field, not a paragraph added to a\n123: lesson. `.claude/checks/run.sh` consults it -- an entry suppresses the\n124: finding it names, and every entry -- file length, per-function lints,\n125: bare-allow -- is re-checked against the whole tree on every run, so one\n126: that stops firing (the function shrank, the file split, the allow was\n127: deleted) is itself reported as `stale-sinkhole-entry` instead of quietly\n128: outliving its reason. `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it would excuse is itself\n129: a finding (`bare-allow`); the sinkhole is the only sanctioned home. See the\n130: file's own header comment for the entry format, and `check.rs`'s three\n131: entries -- file length, `check()`, `synth()` -- as the worked example.\n132: \n133: ## The vocabulary\n134: \n135: **Check** -- a program that reads the code and emits findings. Clippy is one;\n136: the file-length check is another. Adding a check means adding a finding kind.\n137: \n138: **Finding** -- one named complaint about one place in the code. The name is\n139: the key to its lesson.\n140: \n141: **Lesson** -- a node in the bundle under `lints/`. Missing, thin or unhelpful\n142: all mean the same thing: escalate.\n143: \n144: **Budget** -- a number in `.claude/checks/limits.toml` or `clippy.toml` that a\n145: check compares against. Applies to prose and scripts as well as code. Lowered\n146: deliberately, never to make a finding go away.\n\n(End of file - total 146 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntype: Playbook\nname: code-style\ntitle: Reacting to a code-style finding\ndescription: How to react when the code-style checks report a finding. Invoked by the Stop hook's message, not proactively; read it when a finding names it.\ntags: [code-style, hooks, lessons]\n---\n\n# Reacting to a code-style finding\n\nA check ran over the files this session touched and found something. Each\nfinding names a **kind**, and each kind has a **lesson**: a short file of\nworked examples recording how this repo has decided to handle it.\n\n```\nfile-too-long  src/emit_llvm.rs\n  1666 lines excluding inline tests, budget is 1000 (inherited, already 1666 at merge-base)\n  lesson: .claude/skills/code-style/lints/file-too-long.md\n```\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/.claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md","text":"---\ntype: Playbook\nname: code-style\ntitle: Reacting to a code-style finding\ndescription: How to react when the code-style checks report a finding. Invoked by the Stop hook's message, not proactively; read it when a finding names it.\ntags: [code-style, hooks, lessons]\n---\n\n# Reacting to a code-style finding\n\nA check ran over the files this session touched and found something. Each\nfinding names a **kind**, and each kind has a **lesson**: a short file of\nworked examples recording how this repo has decided to handle it.\n\n```\nfile-too-long  src/emit_llvm.rs\n  1666 lines excluding inline tests, budget is 1000 (inherited, already 1666 at merge-base)\n  lesson: .claude/skills/code-style/lints/file-too-long.md\n```\n\nThe lessons are an [Open Knowledge Format](/.claude/skills/code-style/lints/README.md)\nbundle: plain markdown that cross-links into a graph. Start at the index\nthere. The bundle currently ships empty, on purpose; the index says why. While\nit is empty, every finding leads here, and from here to *Escalate*.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. **Read the lesson.** If the file is missing, skip to *Escalate*.\n2. **Follow its links.** A lesson is a small node, not a manual. It will point\n   at more specific ones; the prose around a link says what it is for.\n3. **Find the case that matches your situation.** Some cases carry a note\n   saying they also fit other situations, or that a combination does.\n4. **If one matches, follow it** and fix the finding.\n5. **If none matches -- or what you found is too thin, too vague, or does not\n   actually tell you what to do here -- escalate.** Do not improvise.\n\n## Escalate\n\nEscalating is the normal outcome for anything new. It is not a failure, and it\nis not something to avoid by picking the nearest example and hoping.\n\n**In a delegated session, escalate by filing a GitHub issue for the\ncoordinator -- never by waiting for a human.** The maintainer interacts only\nwith the drive loop's coordinator, not with delegated sessions, so an\n`AskUserQuestion` in a delegated session parks it forever. Instead: file an\nissue (`gh issue create`) stating what fired, why the lessons do not settle\nit, and the real alternatives with what each costs -- \"split by\nresponsibility, or by kind of item, or leave it and record the exemption?\" is\nan escalation; \"how should I fix this?\" is not. Then take the most\nconservative continuation (leave the finding standing and the code as it is),\ncommit everything that IS settled, and end the turn. The coordinator triages\nthe issue: answers it where decided design already does, or turns it into a\ndecide row on `plans/board.yaml` for a grilling session.\n\n**Only a session the maintainer is personally driving may `AskUserQuestion`\ndirectly.** A subagent can do neither -- it has no channel at all; it reports\nthe escalation in its result and returns, and its spawner files the issue.\n\nDo not fix the finding, and do not finish the escalated part as if the\nquestion were not there.\n\n## Writing what was settled\n\nWrite the outcome into the lessons bundle. Three rules, in order of\npreference:\n\n1. **Nothing new.** If an existing case already covers the situation, add one\n   line to it saying so. Prefer this.\n2. **A line, not a section.** If a combination of existing cases covers it,\n   say which, in one sentence, where they are.\n3. **A new node.** Only when the situation is genuinely new. Make it a *small\n   file of its own* and link it from the parent lesson -- do not grow the\n   parent. Where a toy-browser lesson holds the same repo-independent insight,\n   cite it as a source, per AGENTS.md's citing rule.\n\nLessons are held to the same length budget as the code. That is deliberate:\nit is what forces specific knowledge into linked nodes instead of one wall of\nprose. Being as specific as you like is exactly what the graph is for.\n\n## The file format\n\nEvery lesson is a valid OKF v0.2 document: YAML frontmatter with at least\n`type`, then markdown.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntype: Playbook\ntitle: Splitting an emitter that grew several jobs\ndescription: What to do when one backend file accumulated unrelated responsibilities.\ntags: [file-too-long, structure]\n---\n\nemit_llvm.rs held codegen, its runtime shims and the type mapping...\n```\n\n- `type` is the only required key. Use `Playbook` for \"how to handle X\",\n  `Reference` for a note others link to.\n- Link with plain markdown, bundle-relative:\n  `[the split we did for check.rs](/.claude/skills/code-style/lints/file-too-long/by-responsibility.md)`.\n  The link is untyped; the sentence around it carries the relationship.\n- Unknown keys are preserved by any conformant consumer, so this file's `name`\n  and `description` -- which the harness needs -- cost nothing.\n\n## Suppressing a finding\n\nSometimes the right answer is that the check is wrong for this case. That is\nalso a decision worth recording: escalate, settle it, and write the exception\ninto the lesson so the next agent does not re-litigate it.\n\nNever silence a finding by editing `limits.toml`, adding an `#[allow]`, or\nsplitting a file into meaningless pieces to get under a number. The budget\ncomes down over time by deliberate commits; it does not move to accommodate\none session.\n\nSuppression is settled the same way as anything else, and almost never comes\nback \"yes\". If you believe a check is measuring nothing here, look for the\nbetter pattern first and bring *that* back -- an exemption is what is left\nwhen the search failed, argued for in the open, never something a session\ndecides on its own to get unblocked.\n\nA settled exemption moves into `.claude/checks/sinkhole.toml`: one entry per\nfinding it excuses, with a `justification` field, not a paragraph added to a\nlesson. `.claude/checks/run.sh` consults it -- an entry suppresses the\nfinding it names, and every entry -- file length, per-function lints,\nbare-allow -- is re-checked against the whole tree on every run, so one\nthat stops firing (the function shrank, the file split, the allow was\ndeleted) is itself reported as `stale-sinkhole-entry` instead of quietly\noutliving its reason. `#[allow]`/`#![allow]` beside the code it would excuse is itself\na finding (`bare-allow`); the sinkhole is the only sanctioned home. See the\nfile's own header comment for the entry format, and `check.rs`'s three\nentries -- file length, `check()`, `synth()` -- as the worked example.\n\n## The vocabulary\n\n**Check** -- a program that reads the code and emits findings. Clippy is one;\nthe file-length check is another. Adding a check means adding a finding kind.\n\n**Finding** -- one named complaint about one place in the code. The name is\nthe key to its lesson.\n\n**Lesson** -- a node in the bundle under `lints/`. Missing, thin or unhelpful\nall mean the same thing: escalate.\n\n**Budget** -- a number in `.claude/checks/limits.toml` or `clippy.toml` that a\ncheck compares against. Applies to prose and scripts as well as code. Lowered\ndeliberately, never to make a finding go away.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":146,"totalLines":146,"truncated":false}},"title":".claude/skills/code-style/SKILL.md","time":{"start":1788300831483,"end":1788300831497}},"id":"prt_05f09281f001TS9w18g1axdeX0","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f0911de001J0nuwDYQcGRV5q"}}
