{"type":"text","timestamp":1788300888459,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05f0a00dd0018SBx5ObRv64H0v","messageID":"msg_05f092b29001aofoisAc6DOKJg","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","type":"text","text":"The Stop hook will report the pre-existing warning on this touched function regardless; my +1 makes it worse. Let me measure extract's complexity precisely to pick the cleanest layout. Trying clippy's config override:\n\n","time":{"start":1788300886237,"end":1788300888456}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300888543,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-afd5191bbd08a58f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config 'cognitive-complexity-threshold=0' 2>&1 | rg -n -B1 -A2 \"docs.rs\"","timeout":300000},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config 'cognitive-complexity-threshold=0' 2>&1 | rg -n -B1 -A2 \"docs.rs\"","time":{"start":1788300888442,"end":1788300888540}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Important context: The code-style SKILL says that when a check finds something on a touched file, I should read the lesson, and if nothing settles it, escalate by filing a GitHub issue (in a delegated session) — but my brief says `gh issue list/search` are denied, only `gh issue view` is allowed. Also the brief says never wait for a human.\n\nBut wait — is the cognitive-complexity warning on `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` even a NEW finding I caused? No — it's pre-existing (13/10 at baseline, 14/10 now). The Stop hook would report it as a finding on tests/docs.rs (a touched file), with the detail \"fn every_fragment_is_a_real_program: the function has a cognitive complexity of (14/10)\". Since this function already exceeded at baseline, the check reports it regardless. The check doesn't distinguish caused vs inherited for clippy findings (only file-too-long has origin tracking).\n\nSo regardless of whether I make it 13 or 14, the Stop hook will report this finding because tests/docs.rs is a touched file and the function is over budget. Hmm. So the question is: does making it 14 vs 13 matter for the Stop hook? Either way it fires. So the \"clean on code you touched\" for clippy can't be achieved while touching this function without either (a) sinking it (which requires escalation/decision, and it's pre-existing debt not mine), or (b) refactoring to get under 10.\n\nWait — but this is pre-existing debt on a function I'm necessarily touching (the gate must live in the docs test). Refactoring `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` to get under 10 would be a big change to a function I shouldn't gratuitously rewrite (AGENTS.md: \"Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch\"). And the complexity is inherent to the 7-backend agreement machinery.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Actually, the cleanest path to genuinely reduce complexity: extract the per-fragment check into a helper function, which would move the complexity OUT of `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` into a new helper. Then `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` drops below 10, and the helper might be over 10 (but then it's a new function with a new warning...).\n\nWait, let me think about this differently. What's the actual goal?\n1. `just test` / `just check` green with checker enforcement intact. ✓ (done)\n2. `just fmt` and `just clippy` \"clean on code you touched\".\n\nFor clippy: the Stop hook fires on tests/docs.rs because of the pre-existing 13/10 warning on a function I touched. This is \"inherited\" debt, but the hook doesn't know that. The code-style skill says: if nothing settles it, escalate by filing a GitHub issue. But I can't file issues (denied: only `gh issue view` allowed). And the brief says never stop to wait for a human, and write ESCALATION.md + take the most conservative continuation if something needs deciding.\n\nHold on — let me reconsider whether I can actually get the function under budget. Let me look at what the function does and whether a natural extraction exists that reduces its complexity. The function:\n- collects fragments\n- loops over them, with slow-check, then a 4-way outcome match\n- asserts failures empty\n\nThe natural extraction: a `fn check_fragment(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String>` that returns failures for one fragment. Then the loop becomes trivial. Let me estimate the helper's complexity. Actually — wait, I realize the honest and minimal thing might be different.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole situation. The repo ALREADY has this warning standing at 13/10 on this exact function at baseline (before any of my changes, on the committed HEAD). This means:\n- At baseline, `just clippy` is NOT clean (the repo's clippy has standing warnings, documented in clippy.toml as intentional).\n- The Stop hook at baseline, for any session touching tests/docs.rs, would report this warning.\n\nSo \"just clippy clean on code you touched\" is aspirational in this repo; the pre-existing warning is not something I created. My job is to not add NEW warnings. Going 13→14 is +1 on an existing warning.\n\nGiven the constraint that I can't file issues and shouldn't refactor a function I wasn't asked to touch (and the refactor to get under 10 would be substantial and risky), the most conservative correct move: implement the gate in a way that keeps the complexity at the baseline 13 (not 14), so I add zero new debt. That's achievable with the `Outcome::Gated` match-arm approach (match arms not counted) — but as analyzed, that moves the `if` into extract (risking a new warning there) unless I use a tiny helper.\n\nLet me just go with the helper approach which is provably clean:\n- `capital_variant_gate(at)` — no control structure beyond matches!(...) (matches! is a macro, not counted).\n- A tiny `gated` transform helper OR — even simpler — put the gate logic entirely within the Gated outcome arm via a helper `gated_failures`, and decide gated-ness at Fragment construction.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. Actually, the simplest zero-complexity-add solution:\n\nKeep my current structure but change the `if capital_variant_gate(...)` early-continue into part of the `gated_failures` dispatch that doesn't add a branch to the loop... \n\nActually — the cleanest: make the loop call a helper `fragment_failures(f)` that encapsulates BOTH the gate and the outcome dispatch. Then `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` loses the big match (massive complexity reduction), and `fragment_failures` gets the match. Let me count `fragment_failures`:\n\n```rust\nfn fragment_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {        // +1\n        return gated_failures(f);\n    }\n    match &f.outcome {                       // +1\n        Outcome::Output(want) => support::agreement_failures(&f.at, &f.program, f.input.as_deref(), &Expect::Output(want.clone())),\n        Outcome::Refusal => {\n            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {   // +1 (nesting 1 → 2)\n                vec![format!(\"BROKEN ...\")]\n            } else {\n                support::agreement_failures(..., &Expect::Refusal)\n            }\n        }\n        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {   // +1 (nesting 1 → 2)\n            Ok(_) => vec![...],\n            Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => vec![...],\n            Err(_) => Vec::new(),\n        },\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, wait, but this changes more code than necessary and relocates the complexity. Let me count: `fragment_failures` ≈ 1 (if) + 1 (match) + 2 (if-else in Refusal) + 2 (inner match) + guard (Err(e) if — the guard counts +1?) ≈ 7-8. Under 10. And `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` becomes:\n\n```rust\nfor f in &fragments {\n    if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {    // +1+1\n        failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n        continue;\n    }\n    failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n}\n```\n≈ 2-3 complexity. \n\nBut this is a substantial refactor of `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` (moving the whole outcome match to a helper). AGENTS.md says \"Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch, and don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\" This would be folding a refactor into my change. But it's a small, well-scoped extraction that directly serves the task (adding the gate) AND fixes a pre-existing lint on a function I must touch. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — let me step back. I keep going around in circles. Let me re-read the brief's definition of done precisely:\n\n\"Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root ...; `just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on code you touched; work committed on this branch per AGENTS.md ...\"\n\nThe intent: my touched code shouldn't leave new lint debt. The gate is the change. The pre-existing 13/10 warning on every_fragment is inherited debt. My current version makes it 14/10 — that's +1 caused debt.\n\nLet me go with the extraction approach (fragment_failures helper). It:\n1. Keeps every_fragment under budget (fixes the inherited warning too, since I'm here).\n2. Adds a new helper under budget.\n3. Naturally accommodates the gate.\n4. Is a modest, well-scoped extraction.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the extraction is \"folding a style cleanup into a behavior change\" in a way that violates AGENTS.md. The behavior change IS the gate. The extraction is a structural consequence: with the gate, the loop body has one more branch; extracting makes the gate clean. I think a moderate extraction is defensible and actually makes the gate clearer. But it does touch more of the function than the minimal gate.\n\nAlternative minimal approach that keeps every_fragment at 13/10 (baseline, no +1): the `Outcome::Gated` variant + gate-at-construction. Let me carefully verify extract's complexity doesn't increase with the helper `gated`:\n\nextract current: I need its actual complexity number to be sure. Let me measure it. I'll temporarily set the threshold very low via clippy.toml edit... no. \n\nHmm, let me try yet another measurement approach: `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -W clippy::cognitive-complexity` doesn't help. Let me check if clippy supports `--config` at the cargo level... `cargo clippy` passes args after `--` to clippy-driver, which accepts `--config`? Clippy does support `--config` (clippy config). Let me try `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- --config cognitive-complexity-threshold=0`. Earlier it returned nothing — maybe because the flag wasn't recognized and it errored silently into the pipe. Let me check exit code / run without piping.\n\nActually, simpler: temporarily lower the threshold by editing clippy.toml with the edit tool, run clippy, then revert the edit. The edit tool works on files; reverting restores. Risk: forgetting to revert. But I'll be careful. Actually no — let me avoid file churn.\n\nLet me try the `--config` flag properly and check output.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f0a064c00187mMUfwvmmki2y","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f092b29001aofoisAc6DOKJg"}}
