{"type":"text","timestamp":1788300958115,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"id":"prt_05f0afa2d001u4LjLyzIk0h4F4","messageID":"msg_05f0a190f001SVjN36gMclHu9u","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","type":"text","text":"Clippy doesn't accept `--config`. Rather than hand-count, I'll restructure: extract the per-fragment outcome dispatch into a helper that also carries the gate, which both removes my +1 and fixes the pre-existing 13/10 warning on this touched function. Let me rewrite the loop:\n\n","time":{"start":1788300950061,"end":1788300958111}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788300958137,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8b856491189ed679","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs","newString":"    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n    }\n\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {","oldString":"    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n            failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        match &f.outcome {\n            Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                &f.at,\n                &f.program,\n                f.input.as_deref(),\n                &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n            )),\n            Outcome::Refusal => {\n                // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n                // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n                if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                    failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n                } else {\n                    failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                        &f.at,\n                        &f.program,\n                        f.input.as_deref(),\n                        &Expect::Refusal,\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n            Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n                    f.at\n                )),\n                Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n                    f.at,\n                    e.to_string()\n                )),\n                Err(_) => {}\n            },\n        }\n    }\n\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n@@ -95,50 +95,13 @@\n \n let mut failures = Vec::new();\n \n for f in &fragments {\n-    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n-        failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n-        continue;\n-    }\n     if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n         failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n         continue;\n     }\n-    match &f.outcome {\n-        Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n-            &f.at,\n-            &f.program,\n-            f.input.as_deref(),\n-            &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n-        )),\n-        Outcome::Refusal => {\n-            // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n-            // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n-            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n-                failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n-            } else {\n-                failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n-                    &f.at,\n-                    &f.program,\n-                    f.input.as_deref(),\n-                    &Expect::Refusal,\n-                ));\n-            }\n-        }\n-        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n-            Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n-                \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n-                f.at\n-            )),\n-            Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n-                \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n-                f.at,\n-                e.to_string()\n-            )),\n-            Err(_) => {}\n-        },\n-    }\n+    failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n }\n \n for (at, id) in &embedded {\n     if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-156/tests/docs.rs\n@@ -95,50 +95,13 @@\n \n let mut failures = Vec::new();\n \n for f in &fragments {\n-    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n-        failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n-        continue;\n-    }\n     if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n         failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n         continue;\n     }\n-    match &f.outcome {\n-        Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n-            &f.at,\n-            &f.program,\n-            f.input.as_deref(),\n-            &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n-        )),\n-        Outcome::Refusal => {\n-            // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n-            // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n-            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n-                failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n-            } else {\n-                failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n-                    &f.at,\n-                    &f.program,\n-                    f.input.as_deref(),\n-                    &Expect::Refusal,\n-                ));\n-            }\n-        }\n-        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n-            Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n-                \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n-                f.at\n-            )),\n-            Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n-                \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n-                f.at,\n-                e.to_string()\n-            )),\n-            Err(_) => {}\n-        },\n-    }\n+    failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n }\n \n for (at, id) in &embedded {\n     if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {\n","additions":1,"deletions":38},"truncated":false},"title":"tests/docs.rs","time":{"start":1788300958126,"end":1788300958132}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"As suspected, clippy-driver (rustc-version 1.95.0?) doesn't accept `--config`. Clippy config is read from clippy.toml only (or `CLIPPY_CONF_DIR` env). \n\nLet me use the `CLIPPY_CONF_DIR` env var — but wait, \"environment-variable-prefixed commands (VAR=x cmd) never match the allow-list\" is denied. Hmm. So I can't use CLIPPY_CONF_DIR.\n\nAlternative: temporarily edit clippy.toml threshold to 0, run clippy to get extract's number, then revert. This is a legitimate measurement. Let me do it carefully with edit tool, then revert.\n\nActually — I realize there's a much simpler empirical path. Let me just decide based on reasoning and implement the extraction approach (fragment_failures), then measure the resulting clippy output. If both every_fragment and the new helper are under 10, the extraction approach is clearly best and I'm done worrying. If the helper is over 10, I'll reconsider.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the extraction approach's helper complexity once more, carefully:\n\n```rust\nfn fragment_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n        return gated_failures(f);\n    }\n    match &f.outcome {\n        Outcome::Output(want) => {\n            support::agreement_failures(&f.at, &f.program, f.input.as_deref(), &Expect::Output(want.clone()))\n        }\n        Outcome::Refusal => {\n            // Compiling is checked first and separately...\n            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                vec![format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at)]\n            } else {\n                support::agreement_failures(&f.at, &f.program, f.input.as_deref(), &Expect::Refusal)\n            }\n        }\n        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n            Ok(_) => vec![format!(\n                \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n                f.at\n            )],\n            Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => vec![format!(\n                \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n                f.at,\n                e.to_string()\n            )],\n            Err(_) => Vec::new(),\n        },\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCognitive complexity of fragment_failures:\n- `if capital_variant_gate(&f.at)` → +1\n- `match &f.outcome` → +1\n- `Outcome::Refusal` arm: `if let Err(e)` at nesting depth 2 → +1 + 1(nesting) = 2... \n\nWait, let me get the nesting right. Top-level if: nesting 0, +1. Then match: nesting 1 (inside... no, the match is at top level, after the if-return). Actually the match is a sibling of the if (the if returns, then the match). So match at nesting 0 → +1. Inside the Refusal arm, `if let` at nesting level 1 (inside the match) → +1 + 1 = 2. Inside the Error arm, inner `match` at nesting 1 → +1 + 1 = 2. The guard `Err(e) if ...` → guards count? From clippy docs, cognitive complexity counts: \"guards\" — yes, a guard adds +1 (plus nesting). The guard on the inner match arm at nesting 2 → +1 + 2 = 3? Hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nTotal estimate: 1 (if) + 1 (match) + 2 (if-let) + 2 (inner match) + ~1-3 (guard) ≈ 7-9. Probably under 10, but the guard could push it. The baseline function was 13 with: for(+1+? ) + if slow(+2) + match(+2) + if-let in refusal(+3) + inner match(+2) + guard(+? ). That totals around 13. So the guard is significant.\n\nFor fragment_failures: if(1) + match(1) + if-let(2) + inner-match(2) + guard(~2-3) ≈ 8-9. Under 10 probably.\n\nAnd every_fragment becomes:\n```rust\nfn every_fragment_is_a_real_program() {\n    let mut fragments = Vec::new();\n    let mut embedded = Vec::new();\n    for (page, text) in pages() {       // for: +1\n        extract(&page, &text, &mut fragments, &mut embedded);\n    }\n    assert!(!fragments.is_empty(), ...);\n    assert!(fragments.iter().any(|f| f.slow), ...);\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n    for f in &fragments {               // for: +1\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {    // if: +1, nesting 1 → +2\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n    }\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {         // for: +1\n        if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {   // if: +1, nesting 1 → +2\n            failures.push(...);\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(failures.is_empty(), ...);\n}\n```\nComplexity: for(1) + for(1) + if(2) + for(1) + if(2) = 7. Under 10! Great.\n\nBut wait — the `f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled()` — the `&&` between two conditions. Cognitive complexity counts binary operator sequences: `a && b` = the second operand's `&&` adds +1? Actually cognitive complexity: \"Each binary operator that joins conditions adds +1 to the complexity, except for the first.\" Hmm: \"increment for each of the following: ... sequences of binary logical operators — a && b && c = +2\". For `a && b` = +1. So `f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled()` = +1 for the `&&`. Plus the if +1 + nesting. That adds up.\n\nHmm, the original function's 13 already includes all these. Let me not hand-compute; let me just implement the extraction and measure with clippy.\n\nWait, actually, I realize I should double-check: is the extraction approach going to keep behavior identical? Yes — it's a pure move of the outcome-handling into a helper, plus the gate added inside the helper. The slow-check stays in the loop.\n\nBut hold on — order matters: currently the gate check is BEFORE the slow check (my current code). With extraction, the gate goes inside fragment_failures, which is called AFTER the slow check. So a slow+gated fragment would go through compile_only_failures. Since no gated fragment is slow, behavior is identical for all current fragments. Fine.\n\nActually, wait — I want to reconsider whether to even do the extraction. Let me re-read AGENTS.md's \"Don't reformat code you weren't asked to touch, and don't fold a style cleanup into a behavior change.\" The extraction is arguably a style cleanup folded into a behavior change. But it also directly reduces caused debt and makes the gate cleaner. Given the Stop hook WILL report the (inherited) warning on the touched file, and my goal is \"clippy clean on code you touched\", the extraction is the way to actually achieve a clean clippy on this file.\n\nHmm, but actually — even with the extraction, the Stop hook won't be \"clean\" because... let me think. After extraction, is there ANY clippy warning on tests/docs.rs? If fragment_failures is under 10 and every_fragment is under 10, then no cognitive-complexity warning fires on tests/docs.rs. Are there other warnings? Let me check the current clippy output for docs.rs — only the one cognitive-complexity warning. So after extraction, tests/docs.rs would be clippy-clean. That's the definition-of-done goal.\n\nBut wait — will the extraction introduce the `too_many_lines` lint? too-many-lines-threshold = 100. tests/docs.rs is ~380 lines currently; the function every_fragment is the concern. The lint `too_many_lines` fires per function with >100 non-comment lines. every_fragment after extraction is small. fragment_failures is ~35 lines. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check: does the extraction change where the `// Compiling is checked first and separately...` comment goes? That comment is inside the Refusal arm — I'll keep it.\n\nOK let me implement the extraction. Let me rewrite the loop and add the helper.\n\nCurrent loop (lines 92-131 in my edited file):\n```rust\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n            failures.extend(gated_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        match &f.outcome {\n            Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                &f.at,\n                &f.program,\n                f.input.as_deref(),\n                &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n            )),\n            Outcome::Refusal => {\n                // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n                // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n                if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                    failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n                } else {\n                    failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                        &f.at,\n                        &f.program,\n                        f.input.as_deref(),\n                        &Expect::Refusal,\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n            Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n                    f.at\n                )),\n                Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n                    f.at,\n                    e.to_string()\n                )),\n                Err(_) => {}\n            },\n        }\n    }\n\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {\n        if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"MISSING {at}: embeds corpus case `{id}`, which does not exist\"\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n```\n\nNew loop:\n```rust\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(fragment_failures(f));\n    }\n\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {\n        ...\n    }\n```\n\nAnd the new helper `fragment_failures`:\n```rust\n/// The failures one fragment accumulates: the gated fragments (see `capital_variant_gate`) are\n/// checked for the checker's refusal instead of their stale expectation, and everything else\n/// runs the outcome its fences declared.\nfn fragment_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    if capital_variant_gate(&f.at) {\n        return gated_failures(f);\n    }\n    match &f.outcome {\n        Outcome::Output(want) => support::agreement_failures(\n            &f.at,\n            &f.program,\n            f.input.as_deref(),\n            &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n        ),\n        Outcome::Refusal => {\n            // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n            // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n            if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                vec![format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at)]\n            } else {\n                support::agreement_failures(&f.at, &f.program, f.input.as_deref(), &Expect::Refusal)\n            }\n        }\n        Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n            Ok(_) => vec![format!(\n                \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n                f.at\n            )],\n            Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => vec![format!(\n                \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n                f.at,\n                e.to_string()\n            )],\n            Err(_) => Vec::new(),\n        },\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait — there's a subtlety with the `Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want` guard: `*want` dereferences `&String` to String (clone?) — `*want` where want is `&String`, `*want` is String (would move out of borrow?). Actually in the original, `Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want` — `*want` is a `String` being compared with a `String`; `PartialEq` for String takes references, and `*want` here would be a place expression... `e.to_string() != *want` compares `String` with `String` by auto-ref, no move. It compiled before, so it's fine. I'll keep it identical.\n\nNow — do I need `gated_failures` and `capital_variant_gate` still? Yes. Where to place them? I'll place fragment_failures, capital_variant_gate, gated_failures together, before the `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page` function. Actually, order in the file: currently `compile_only_failures`, then my `capital_variant_gate`, `gated_failures`, then `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page`. I'll insert `fragment_failures` after `compile_only_failures` (since it's the \"main\" per-fragment check), and keep capital_variant_gate/gated_failures after it.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — should fragment_failures come before compile_only_failures? Order doesn't matter in Rust. Let me arrange logically:\n1. `compile_only_failures` (slow tier) — exists.\n2. `fragment_failures` — the main dispatch.\n3. `capital_variant_gate` — the gate predicate.\n4. `gated_failures` — the gate check.\n5. `every_builtin_has_a_reference_page`.\n\nLet me do the edits now. First, replace the loop. Then move the outcome match into the helper.\n\nLet me be careful with the edit oldString to uniquely match.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f0b004f001eNdnj6YOqpT6u2","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f0a190f001SVjN36gMclHu9u"}}
