{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788301176570,"sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b93a8ca17af3f577","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"just check 2>&1 | tail -5","timeout":300000},"output":"        PASS [   8.940s] (336/338) toylang::corpus every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2\n        PASS [  19.226s] (337/338) toylang::backend_llvm native_agrees_where_it_compiles\n        PASS [  29.388s] (338/338) toylang::backend_rust rust_agrees_where_it_compiles\n────────────\n     Summary [  29.393s] 338 tests run: 338 passed, 2 skipped\n","metadata":{"output":"        PASS [   8.940s] (336/338) toylang::corpus every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2\n        PASS [  19.226s] (337/338) toylang::backend_llvm native_agrees_where_it_compiles\n        PASS [  29.388s] (338/338) toylang::backend_rust rust_agrees_where_it_compiles\n────────────\n     Summary [  29.393s] 338 tests run: 338 passed, 2 skipped\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"just check 2>&1 | tail -5","time":{"start":1788301176564,"end":1788301176567}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The docs test passes. Now let me verify the gate actually trips when a gated fragment starts compiling — i.e., the gate isn't a silent no-op. I want to sanity-check the \"GATED\" failure path works. \n\nI could do this by temporarily changing a gated fragment to compile... but I must not touch doc content. Alternatively, I can write a tiny throwaway test in a scratch file and run it... but I can't easily run a scratch without it being part of the test suite, and I can't delete files. Hmm.\n\nActually, a clean way: I can verify the gate logic via the existing `just check` by temporarily... no.\n\nLet me think — is there value in testing the negative path? The gate logic is simple and I'm confident. But per AGENTS.md \"Every fixed bug gets the test that would have caught it\" and \"Don't assert things that cannot fail\" — the gate's `gated_failures` asserting the casing message is itself an assertion that can fail. And I verified from the live run (before gating) that all 11 fragments produce exactly that message. So the positive path is verified.\n\nFor the negative path (does the gate trip when a gated fragment compiles?), I could add a unit test to tests/docs.rs... but that would require constructing a Fragment and calling gated_failures — the tests/docs.rs is a single integration test binary with two #[test] functions. Adding a third test for the gate's trip behavior might be over-engineering, and it'd need a Fragment instance with a fake `at`. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The gate exists to be REMOVED by doc-migrate-capital-variants. Its correct behavior is: green now, red if a gated fragment starts compiling (which would signal the migration is needed). The \"red\" path is exercised only in that future. Testing it now is reasonable but adds test surface to a file that's otherwise just the two harness tests. I'll skip adding a dedicated test — the gate is transparent (a match arm calling gated_failures) and the positive path is verified. Over-testing a temporary gate isn't worth it.\n\nHmm, but wait — let me reconsider one risk: what if one of the 11 gated fragments currently fails for a DIFFERENT reason than casing (e.g., it's actually MISQUOTED because of the coverage error but ALSO would trip the gate's casing check)? Let me re-examine: all 11 produce \"a variant name starts with a capital letter\" per LAND-FAILURE.txt and the live run. So the gate passes for all 11 via the casing message. Confirmed.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the MISQUOTED fragments (matching.md:54, enum.md:34, enum.md:67, 06-matching.md:67). These have `error` fences with stale text. My gate replaces their check with \"must still be refused for the casing rule\". Good — they currently ARE refused for the casing rule. Verified.\n\nNow let me run `just check` to confirm the full fast suite is still green after the refactor, then `just fmt`, then commit.\n\nActually — `just check` compiles and runs 338 tests (~30s). Let me run it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05f0df840001ImiPhxH1abL7lz","sessionID":"ses_fa1053b46ffegpZct5fvxttIRv","messageID":"msg_05f0daa71001EbvAz8Ujj6msLz"}}
